WEBVTT

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:03.500
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>来自芝加哥WBEZ广播电台，这是《美国生活》。

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:14.200
<v Rochelle Garza>Step one, you are currently in the custody of the US department of Homeland Security Customs and Border Protection. You have been charged with the crime of illegal entry into the United States. Step two--
<v Rochelle Garza>第一步，你目前正处于美国国土安全部海关及边境保卫局的拘留之下。你已被指控犯有非法入境美国的罪行。第二步——

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:17.150
<v Ira Glass>So Kevin, who is this and what is she reading?
<v Ira Glass>凯文，这是谁？她在读什么？

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:25.790
<v Kevin Sieff>This is Rachelle Garza. She's an immigration attorney in Brownsville, Texas and she's reading a flyer that was handed to some parents after they were separated from their children.
<v Kevin Sieff>这是雷切尔·加尔萨。她是德克萨斯州布朗斯维尔的一名移民律师，她正在阅读一份传单，这份传单是在一些父母与孩子被迫分离后发给他们的。

00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:28.360
<v Ira Glass>Kevin Sieff is correspondent for The Washington Post.
<v Ira Glass>凯文·谢夫是《华盛顿邮报》的记者。

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:39.770
<v Kevin Sieff>And on the flyer there's a phone number, basically, which is really the only lifeline they have to their children-- the only way they can get information from the government about where their children are, how their children are doing.
<v Kevin Sieff>传单上有一个电话号码，基本上，这是他们与孩子取得联系的唯一生命线——这是他们从政府那里获知孩子下落及近况的唯一途径。

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:42.060
<v Ira Glass>And explain what she's trying to do.
<v Ira Glass>解释一下她想做什么。

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:53.540
<v Kevin Sieff>She's trying to locate her client's child. So she represents a man who is detained in an immigration detention center who, basically minutes after crossing the border, was separated from his daughter.
<v Kevin Sieff>她想找到她客户的孩子。她代理了一名被关押在移民拘留中心的男子，他在越境后仅仅几分钟就与女儿分开了。

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:55.190
<v Ira Glass>How old is his daughter?
<v Ira Glass>他女儿多大？

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:56.450
<v Kevin Sieff>She's 12
<v Kevin Sieff>她12岁。

00:00:56.450 --> 00:01:13.460
<v Rochelle Garza>OK, so let's figure out how we're supposed to do this. So action one, call ICE. If you're in a detention facility, call this way. OK, let's call the ORR parent hotline.
<v Rochelle Garza>好吧，那我们来看看该怎么做。第一步，致电美国移民及海关执法局（ICE）。如果你在拘留设施内，请这样拨打。好的，我们来拨打难民安置办公室（ORR）的家长热线。

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:15.770
<v Ira Glass>What is that, the ORR parent hotline?
<v Ira Glass>那是什么，ORR家长热线？

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:33.870
<v Kevin Sieff>So, that's the phone number that you're supposed to call, either as a parent or as an attorney, to try to locate your child. I mean, it's really the most-- when you look at this flyer, it's the most useful piece of information on it. So yeah, this is the number that Rochelle's about to call.
<v Kevin Sieff>那是你作为家长或律师应该拨打的电话号码，目的是尝试找到你的孩子。我的意思是，当你看着这张传单时，这是上面最有用的信息。没错，这就是雷切尔马上要拨打的号码。

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:34.360
<v Kevin Sieff>[PHONE RINGING]
<v Kevin Sieff>[电话铃声]

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:39.280
<v Woman>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Woman>[西班牙语对话]

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.170
<v Kevin Sieff>So it starts in Spanish, and then it switches to English.
<v Kevin Sieff>所以它先用西班牙语，然后切换到英语。

00:01:42.170 --> 00:02:07.090
<v Woman>You have reached the ORR national call center, open 24 hours a day, seven days a week. If you are calling about a person travelling to the United States who is 18 years of age or older, please press one. If you have a case manager's extension, please press two. For all other calls, please press three or remain on the line and you will be connected with a case manager. Your call will be monitored for quality assurance.
<v Woman>你已接通ORR全国呼叫中心，我们全天24小时，每周七天为您服务。如果你拨打此电话是为了查询一位年满18周岁或以上的前往美国的人员，请按1。如果你有案件经理的分机号，请按2。如需其他咨询，请按3或保持在线，我们将为你连接一位案件经理。你的通话将被录音以保证服务质量。

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:10.530
<v Woman>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Woman>[音乐播放]

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:15.810
<v Ira Glass>OK, so there's hold music and there's a phone tree. This all feels very, like-- the most normal thing in the world.
<v Ira Glass>好的，有等待音乐和自动语音菜单。这感觉非常……非常正常。

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:18.390
<v Kevin Sieff>Exactly. It's like you're trying to fix your cable.
<v Kevin Sieff>没错。就像你在尝试修复有线电视一样。

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:23.600
<v Ira Glass>So on this recording you made, an operator picks up after six minutes-- not that long, really.
<v Ira Glass>在你录制的这段录音中，接线员在六分钟后接听了——其实并没有那么久。

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:26.870
<v Woman>Please wait as we connect--
<v Woman>请稍候，我们正在为你转接——

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:30.460
<v Alan>[SPEAKING SPANISH]?
<v Alan>[西班牙语对话]？

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:33.830
<v Rochelle Garza>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Rochelle Garza>[西班牙语对话]

00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:34.890
<v Alan>Yes, of course.
<v Alan>是的，当然。

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:36.650
<v Rochelle Garza>OK, how are you doing Alan?
<v Rochelle Garza>好的，艾伦，你好吗？

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:38.280
<v Alan>Pretty good.
<v Alan>挺好的。

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:41.970
<v Rochelle Garza>So, my name is Rochelle Garza. I'm an attorney.
<v Rochelle Garza>我叫雷切尔·加尔萨。我是一名律师。

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:42.620
<v Alan>OK.
<v Alan>好的。

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:53.850
<v Rochelle Garza>I'm looking for my client's daughter. I called last week, on the 11th of June, and I was told that I would be contacted by the shelter.
<v Rochelle Garza>我在找我客户的女儿。我上周，也就是6月11日打过电话，当时我被告知避难所会联系我。

00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:54.720
<v Alan>The shelter?
<v Alan>避难所？

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:59.190
<v Rochelle Garza>Yeah. And I haven't been contacted, and neither has my client.
<v Rochelle Garza>是的。但我没有收到联系，我的客户也没有。

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:02.370
<v Alan>OK. What's the name of the minor?
<v Alan>好的。未成年人的名字是什么？

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:07.690
<v Kevin Sieff>So she gives the name of this girl to the operator and he looks her up and finds her in the system.
<v Kevin Sieff>她把女孩的名字告诉了接线员，他查找了系统并找到了她。

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:26.660
<v Alan>What I can do is to try to do-- try to send the shelter inquiry again. I mean, because they usually do contact. But right now, with the high volume of minors that are entering the United States, it's a little complicated for them.
<v Alan>我能做的是尝试——尝试再次向避难所发送查询。因为他们通常会联系。但目前，随着进入美国的未成年人数量激增，对他们来说有点复杂。

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:32.120
<v Ira Glass>Hey Kevin, when he says the high volume of minors entering the United States, is that what's happening?
<v Ira Glass>嘿凯文，当他说进入美国的未成年人数量激增时，事实确实如此吗？

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:45.740
<v Kevin Sieff>No. I mean, there is no surge of minors crossing the border. In fact, there are fewer people crossing the border than almost any time in the last decade. What there's a surge of is a surge of families being separated, and that's what's changed.
<v Kevin Sieff>不。并没有出现未成年人越境的激增。事实上，越境人数比过去十年中的任何时候都要少。真正激增的是被迫分离的家庭数量，这就是变化之处。

00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:47.270
<v Ira Glass>And that's why he's getting so many calls.
<v Ira Glass>这就是为什么他会接到这么多电话的原因。

00:03:47.270 --> 00:03:51.530
<v Kevin Sieff>Right. There are children and parents who can't find each other.
<v Kevin Sieff>对。有太多孩子和父母找不到彼此。

00:03:51.530 --> 00:04:12.070
<v Rochelle Garza>Maybe there's a better way for us to do this. Can I send a letter to you or to the shelter somehow, saying that I'm representing her father? That way I can at least confirm, you know, where she is and so I can go ahead and start working on her case.
<v Rochelle Garza>也许我们可以换种方式。我可以寄一封信给你或避难所吗，说明我代理了她父亲？这样我至少可以确认她的下落，好让我继续处理她的案子。

00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:24.550
<v Alan>OK. For that-- so basically, we don't release that information of the shelter. We can't release that information to the public.
<v Alan>好的。关于这点——基本上，我们不会披露避难所的相关信息。我们不能向公众披露这些信息。

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:28.460
<v Ira Glass>We don't release the information saying what shelter she's in, is what he's saying, right?
<v Ira Glass>他是说他们不会披露女孩所在的避难所信息，对吧？

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:29.280
<v Kevin Sieff>Right.
<v Kevin Sieff>对。

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:44.840
<v Alan>Due to safety concerns, safety purposes. But I mean, I can type out a little bit of, like, whatever you want to tell them, tell the shelter, and I can make sure they get the message if that'll work for you.
<v Alan>出于安全考虑。但我可以帮你打印一份你想转达给避难所的消息，我可以确保他们收到，如果这对你有帮助的话。

00:04:44.840 --> 00:05:11.850
<v Rochelle Garza>Yeah, no. I guess I'm just trying to figure out a way that I can explain that I'm an attorney and that I'm the attorney assisting the parent. And the parent has asked me to locate the child. So that way, I can speak with her. Is there any email address that I can maybe send a letter of representation to? Even if it's to someone at your office.
<v Rochelle Garza>不，不用了。我只是想弄清楚有什么办法能证明我是律师，并且是协助家长的律师。家长已经委托我寻找孩子。这样我就能和她谈谈。有没有什么电子邮件地址可以发送代理委托书？即使是发给你办公室的人也可以。

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:13.940
<v Alan>Give me one second.
<v Alan>请等我一下。

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:20.840
<v Kevin Sieff>I think that's one of the fundamental problems here, is that they're making it really hard for her to establish that she is, in fact, representing a child.
<v Kevin Sieff>我认为这是根本问题之一，他们让证明自己确实在代理某个孩子变得非常困难。

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:25.310
<v Alan>And is the parent in the detention center?
<v Alan>家长在拘留中心吗？

00:05:25.310 --> 00:05:30.770
<v Rochelle Garza>Yes. I think you can probably see that in your system, right?
<v Rochelle Garza>是的。我想你大概可以在系统中看到，对吧？

00:05:30.770 --> 00:05:33.140
<v Alan>I can try to find them. But--
<v Alan>我可以试着找一下。但是——

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:34.210
<v Rochelle Garza>Do you want me to--
<v Rochelle Garza>需要我——

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:35.540
<v Alan>I was just asking so that--
<v Alan>我只是问一下以便——

00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:37.190
<v Rochelle Garza>Do you want me to confirm his name?
<v Rochelle Garza>需要我确认他的名字吗？我可以给你。

00:05:37.190 --> 00:05:37.610
<v Alan>Huh?
<v Alan>嗯？

00:05:37.610 --> 00:05:40.240
<v Rochelle Garza>Do you want me to confirm the dad's name? I can give that to you.
<v Rochelle Garza>需要我确认父亲的名字吗？我可以告诉你。

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:50.650
<v Alan>No, it's OK. No, I only have the information for the minors. I don't have the-- for the adults. I mean, I can't go to the ICE locator and find them, but--
<v Alan>不，没关系。我只有未成年人的信息。我没有成人的信息。我不能去ICE的定位系统查找他们，但是——

00:05:50.650 --> 00:06:00.040
<v Ira Glass>This surprised me. Is he saying that his database lists the child and where the child is, but doesn't have the parent's name attached to that?
<v Ira Glass>这让我很惊讶。他是说他的数据库里列出了孩子及其位置，但没有关联家长的名字？

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:24.060
<v Kevin Sieff>Yeah. I mean, he's telling Rochelle that he has no record of even that parent's name, let alone where that parent's located. I mean, and this, I think, is the biggest concern that lawyers have right now is, is there even a record in a government database of which child belongs to which parent? We don't know the answer to that question. But there are a lot of reasons, including this exchange, that would make us think that they don't. They don't know.
<v Kevin Sieff>是的。他告诉雷切尔，他甚至没有那个家长的记录，更不用说他在哪里了。我想这是目前律师们最担心的问题：政府数据库里是否真的有关于哪个孩子属于哪个家长的记录？我们不知道答案。但有很多理由，包括这次对话，让我们认为他们确实不知道。

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:29.540
<v Ira Glass>So again, so in the recording, he then heads off to try to answer her question about how can she get through to the daughter.
<v Ira Glass>回到录音，他随后去尝试回答她关于如何联系女儿的问题。

00:06:29.540 --> 00:06:38.570
<v Alan>OK, I mean, just give me one second so I can speak to my supervisor about this. Let me ask what else I can do for you.
<v Alan>好的，请给我一分钟，让我和主管商量一下。让我问问还能为你做什么。

00:06:38.570 --> 00:06:40.740
<v Rochelle Garza>OK. Thank you, I appreciate that.
<v Rochelle Garza>好的。谢谢，我很感激。

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:42.190
<v Alan>Do you mind holding on--
<v Alan>介意稍等一下吗——

00:06:42.190 --> 00:06:42.920
<v Rochelle Garza>I do not mind.
<v Rochelle Garza>我不介意。

00:06:42.920 --> 00:06:45.510
<v Alan>--for a little bit? Thank you.
<v Alan>——一会儿吗？谢谢。

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:46.790
<v Alan>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Alan>[音乐播放]

00:06:46.790 --> 00:06:52.910
<v Ira Glass>Kevin, so on his computer screen he actually has the information that she wants, of where this girl is?
<v Ira Glass>凯文，他的电脑屏幕上其实有她想要的那个女孩位置的信息？

00:06:52.910 --> 00:06:59.030
<v Kevin Sieff>Yeah, that's basically what he's saying, that he has it. He knows where she is, but he can't give it to her.
<v Kevin Sieff>是的，他基本上就是这么说的，他有这些信息。他知道她在哪，但他不能告诉她。

00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:07.760
<v Ira Glass>Now eventually, as this call goes on, the guy gives her an email address. But then it's just the same email address that's on the form that she has, right?
<v Ira Glass>随着通话继续，那人给了她一个电子邮箱。但那只是她表格上已有的那个地址，对吧？

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:16.210
<v Kevin Sieff>Yeah. Yeah, and I mean, I kind of saw her just entirely deflated after he gave that address.
<v Kevin Sieff>是的。在他给了那个地址后，我看到她完全泄气了。

00:07:16.210 --> 00:07:21.360
<v Kevin Sieff>Right, so having had it, how do you feel about that call?
<v Kevin Sieff>好吧，既然经历过，你对那次通话感觉如何？

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:23.700
<v Rochelle Garza>Not very good.
<v Rochelle Garza>感觉不太好。

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:27.250
<v Kevin Sieff>Why?
<v Kevin Sieff>为什么？

00:07:27.250 --> 00:07:58.970
<v Rochelle Garza>I mean, they're not really trying to help. Yeah. Sorry, it's rough. I just don't know how we're going to track down these kids. That's not helpful. I mean, I can still go through the motions of sending an email to that email address, but I just don't have any faith that it's going to go anywhere.
<v Rochelle Garza>他们并没有真心想帮忙。抱歉，这很难受。我不知道我们该如何找到这些孩子。这毫无帮助。我可以走个过场给那个地址发邮件，但我根本不相信会有结果。

00:07:58.970 --> 00:08:11.370
<v Rochelle Garza>Sorry, I'm getting emotional. But it's very frustrating. And I can only imagine what's going on on her end.
<v Rochelle Garza>抱歉，我情绪有点激动。但这太让人沮丧了。我只能想象她那边是什么情况。

00:08:11.370 --> 00:08:14.850
<v Ira Glass>OK, so that was a week ago. What's happened since?
<v Ira Glass>好的，那是上周的事了。后来发生了什么？

00:08:14.850 --> 00:08:31.830
<v Kevin Sieff>So, Rochelle reached out to one of the legal service providers who work with children who are detained after crossing the border. And these are organizations that represent children through their deportation proceedings, through their asylum hearings. And these organizations keep track of the kids that they're working with.
<v Kevin Sieff>雷切尔联系了那些为越境后被拘留的儿童提供服务的法律机构之一。这些组织在遣返诉讼和庇护听证期间为儿童提供代理，并会记录他们代理的孩子。

00:08:31.830 --> 00:08:43.410
<v Kevin Sieff>They each have a list of the kids. And Rochelle kind of got lucky, and the person she reached out to did actually have a record of this girl and was able to tell Rochelle where the girl was being held.
<v Kevin Sieff>他们每个人都有孩子们的名单。雷切尔很幸运，她联系的那个人确实有这个女孩的记录，并能够告诉雷切尔她被关押在哪里。

00:08:43.410 --> 00:08:44.820
<v Ira Glass>She found the name of the shelter?
<v Ira Glass>她找到了避难所的名字？

00:08:44.820 --> 00:09:04.320
<v Kevin Sieff>She found the shelter, yeah. So then the next step was for her to get to the shelter to try to set up basically an appointment. So on Thursday afternoon, she drove to the shelter. And she walked in and there the girl was. I talked to her right afterwards.
<v Kevin Sieff>是的，她找到了避难所。下一步就是去避难所预约。周四下午，她开车去了那里。她走进去，女孩就在那。我事后立刻和她谈了。

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:37.290
<v Rochelle Garza>She looked like her dad, so I recognized her right away. I felt-- I felt really emotional about it actually, when I saw her, just because I could see her dad's face in her face. I had to-- obviously, any time you go and you introduce yourself to a child, like they don't know who you are. I obviously explained my relationship to her dad and to her and that I've been looking for her.
<v Rochelle Garza>她长得像她爸爸，所以我一眼就认出她了。我当时感到非常难过，因为我能在她脸上看到她父亲的影子。显然，每次你去向一个孩子介绍自己，她们都不知道你是谁。我当然向她解释了我与她父亲的关系，以及我一直在寻找她。

00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:56.950
<v Rochelle Garza>And her eyes turned really red when I mentioned her dad. And I asked her if she wanted to write him a letter and she was like, yes. And so she frantically wrote this letter, and I gave her the time to do that.
<v Rochelle Garza>当我提到她父亲时，她的眼睛变红了。我问她是否想写信给他，她说想。于是她急切地写了这封信，我给了她时间。

00:09:56.950 --> 00:10:02.190
<v Ira Glass>And so this girl, she's 12 years old. What did Rochelle say? Is she doing OK?
<v Ira Glass>这个女孩12岁。雷切尔怎么说？她还好吗？

00:10:02.190 --> 00:10:23.090
<v Kevin Sieff>Yeah, the girl's doing OK. Rochelle said she looks healthy. She's wearing a uniform given to her, a sort of polo shirt given to her by the shelter. And she was wearing a bunch of friendship bracelets that I guess she's learned to make there. And then Rochelle had to do this thing, which is explain that this girl probably is not going to see her dad any time soon, and she might not even talk to him any time soon.
<v Kevin Sieff>是的，女孩还好。雷切尔说她看起来很健康。她穿着避难所发给她的制服，一种POLO衫。她还戴着很多友谊手链，我想是她在那里学会做的。接着雷切尔必须做这件事，那就是向女孩解释，她近期可能无法见到父亲，甚至可能无法和他通话。

00:10:23.090 --> 00:10:54.330
<v Rochelle Garza>The way you explain it is, like, you kind of have to explain what's going on in the national realm, right? You have to say there was this executive order and there was this decision out of the courts, and put it in that kind of context so that-- I mean the thing is, I don't have an answer for her, Kevin. Like, I can't tell her, you're for sure going to get a phone call this day. You're for sure going to be reunited with him on this day. All I can say is, I don't know.
<v Rochelle Garza>这种解释方式就像是必须解释全国范围内正在发生的事情。你必须说，有这样的行政命令，法院也有这样的裁决，把情况放在那种背景下。问题是，凯文，我没法给她答案。我不能告诉她：“你某天一定会接到电话，你某天一定会和父亲团聚。”我只能说：“我不知道。”

00:10:54.330 --> 00:11:05.000
<v Ira Glass>Kevin, I thought the policy had changed. Like, the president announced that we are no longer separating families from children. And in fact, the government announced that they had reunited over 500 children.
<v Ira Glass>凯文，我以为政策已经改变了。总统宣布我们不再将家庭和儿童分开了。事实上，政府还宣布他们已经使500多名儿童与家人团聚。

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:22.270
<v Kevin Sieff>Yeah, I mean, the government has made these announcements about their plans to reunify families. But when you talk to the lawyers who are representing the parents, nothing has changed at all. And in many cases, not only are the families not reunified, but these lawyers still can't figure out where the children are being held.
<v Kevin Sieff>政府确实宣布了他们的家庭团聚计划。但当你采访代理父母的律师时，情况一点都没变。在很多情况下，不仅家庭没有团聚，律师们甚至仍然无法查明孩子们被关在哪里。

00:11:22.270 --> 00:11:27.190
<v Ira Glass>And what do we know about the 500-plus kids who the government says they reunited?
<v Ira Glass>关于政府声称团聚的那500多名儿童，我们知道什么？

00:11:27.190 --> 00:11:46.250
<v Kevin Sieff>I mean, the government has said that they've reunited these families. When I talk to lawyers who are representing hundreds of parents-- I mean, there's one organization that's representing 376, another that's representing I think over 400 now. I haven't heard of a single-- at least as of a few days ago, I haven't heard of a single reunification.
<v Kevin Sieff>政府说他们已经促成了团聚。但当我采访那些代理数百名父母的律师时——有一个组织代理了376人，另一个似乎超过400人。我没听说过一个——至少几天前我还没听说过任何成功的团聚案例。

00:11:46.250 --> 00:12:17.270
<v Kevin Sieff>So I mean, I think in the absence of concrete information about who these families were, how they were reunified, lawyers are really wondering if these kids were among the 2,300 who were separated from their parents. If maybe they were from a different pool of children. It's possible that the government numbers are accurate, but there are just so many questions about them, because there's really no documentation that goes along with it.
<v Kevin Sieff>所以我认为，在缺乏关于这些家庭是谁、如何团聚的明确信息的情况下，律师们真的很怀疑这些孩子是否在当初分离的2300人之中，还是说他们来自另一批孩子。政府的数据可能是准确的，但由于缺乏相应记录，关于这些数据存在太多疑问。

00:12:17.270 --> 00:12:49.450
<v Ira Glass>We reached out to the Department of Homeland Security for clarification about all of this, and a spokesperson told us that the 539 children they've said were reunited with parents were all apprehended since May, as part of the administration's zero tolerance policy, and they all were in border patrol custody before being returned to their families. Presumably, being in border patrol custody means they were only in the country a short time and had never been transferred to other agencies, shelters, and foster homes. The spokesperson declined to provide any names of these families or any information about what countries they were from.
<v Ira Glass>我们联系了国土安全部寻求澄清，一位发言人告诉我们，他们所说的已团聚的539名儿童都是自五月起，作为“零容忍”政策的一部分被逮捕的，并且在被送还家人前都处于边境巡逻队的羁押下。据推测，处于边境巡逻队羁押意味着他们进入美国的时间很短，从未被转移到其他机构、避难所或寄养家庭。发言人拒绝提供这些家庭的名字，也拒绝提供关于他们来自哪些国家的信息。

00:12:49.450 --> 00:13:13.010
<v Ira Glass>But this kind of situation where somebody-- in this case, the President of the United States-- declares that he's changed-- it's all different, he's moving on, no longer separating kids from their parents, but the result is not so clear. That situation, that is what our show is about today. Something happens, maybe you'd like to believe it's true, but you don't know if you should.
<v Ira Glass>但这种有人——在本案中是美国总统——宣称他已经改变、一切都不同了，他正在前进、不再将孩子与父母分开，但结果却并不明确的情况，正是我们今天节目要探讨的主题。事情发生了，你或许愿意相信它是真的，但你不知道是否应该相信。

00:13:13.010 --> 00:13:35.860
<v Ira Glass>We have a bunch of different stories that do not involve the current administration, or any previous ones. One of the stories, in fact, takes us into a noisy world of half-clad soap opera stars and daytime TV hosts, none of whom I bet you have ever heard of. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>我们还有很多不涉及现任政府或任何前任政府的故事。事实上，其中一个故事把我们带进了一个喧闹的世界，那里满是衣着暴露的肥皂剧明星和日间节目主持人，我敢说你一个都没听说过。请留下来。

00:13:35.860 --> 00:13:54.640
<v Ira Glass>Act One, The All Too Real Housewives of Argentina. There's an old saying, don't believe everything you see. But there's a corollary, of course-- don't believe everything you see on TV, especially daytime TV. And it doesn't matter what country you're in. Jasmine Garsd grew up in Argentina and has this story.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，阿根廷最真实的家庭主妇。有句老话，别相信你看到的每件事。当然还有个推论——别相信电视上看到的每件事，尤其是日间电视节目。无论你在哪个国家都一样。洁丝敏·加尔德在阿根廷长大，她有这个故事。

00:13:54.640 --> 00:14:05.860
<v Ira Glass>Quick warning before we start-- the stories in today's show have some curse words that we have un-beeped here in the podcast version of the show. If you want a version with beeps, maybe you're listening with kids, go to our website. And here's Jasmine.
<v Ira Glass>在开始前做一个简短警告——今天节目中的故事包含一些脏话，我们在播客版中没有消音。如果你想要消音版，或者你正带着孩子收听，请去我们的网站。这是洁丝敏。

00:14:05.860 --> 00:14:23.070
<v Jasmine Garsd>I watched a lot of television when I was a kid. My grandmother, Iaia, would pick me up at school and bring me back to her place. Her apartment was dark and humid. It smelled like French bread and the exhaust from the buses on the avenue down below. My grandfather was never around.
<v Jasmine Garsd>我小时候看很多电视。我的祖母，也就是Iaia，会从学校接我回家。她的公寓阴暗潮湿。闻起来有法棍面包味，还有楼下大街上巴士的尾气味。我的祖父从不在家。

00:14:23.070 --> 00:14:34.700
<v Jasmine Garsd>Iaia would make tea and then we would go to her bedroom and turn the TV on. And suddenly, colors, sound, and sex would pour into the world. It was the early afternoon. It was time for the talk shows.
<v Jasmine Garsd>Iaia会泡茶，然后我们去卧室打开电视。瞬间，色彩、声音和色情涌入世界。那是午后。是谈话节目的时间。

00:14:34.700 --> 00:14:38.890
<v Man>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Man>[西班牙语对话]

00:14:38.890 --> 00:15:00.370
<v Jasmine Garsd>Argentine talk shows are extreme, even for Latin American television. The women are pumped up with silicone and Botox, and sometimes show up wearing almost nothing. The conversation is not just double entendres, but straight up entendres, full-on vulgar language. When I was growing up, it was a parade of pasties, stilettos, feather boas.
<v Jasmine Garsd>阿根廷的谈话节目极其夸张，即便在拉丁美洲电视圈也是如此。女人们注射了大量硅胶和肉毒杆菌，有时出场时几乎什么都没穿。谈话内容不仅有双关语，更是直白的、充满庸俗语言的内容。在我成长过程中，那简直是一场乳贴、高跟鞋和羽毛围巾的游行。

00:15:00.370 --> 00:15:15.820
<v Jasmine Garsd>One of the most popular shows back then was hosted by a guy named Jorge Rial. He's still on TV. He's kind of the Argentine everyman-- charming and a little bit of a hustler. These days, his TV show is called Intrusos, or Intruders.
<v Jasmine Garsd>当时最火的节目之一是由一个叫豪尔赫·里亚尔的人主持的。他现在还在电视上。他就像阿根廷的平民偶像——迷人，带点投机色彩。这些年，他的节目叫《Intrusos》，意为《入侵者》。

00:15:15.820 --> 00:15:31.270
<v Jasmine Garsd>It takes place on a set that is just seizure inducing-- neon colors, walls lined with giant video screens. Jorge Rial likes to stir up fights among his voluptuous guests. Every time something shocking is said, ominous music rolls out.
<v Jasmine Garsd>节目的场景布置极其刺激感官——霓虹灯色，墙上挂着巨大的视频屏幕。豪尔赫·里亚尔喜欢在他那些丰满的嘉宾中挑起争斗。每次说出什么令人震惊的话，就会配上不祥的音乐。

00:15:31.270 --> 00:15:32.610
<v Woman>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Woman>[西班牙语对话]

00:15:32.610 --> 00:15:36.800
<v Woman>[OMINOUS MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Woman>[不祥的音乐播放]

00:15:36.800 --> 00:16:02.070
<v Jasmine Garsd>Once in a while, a woman is so sexy that Jorge Rial bites his lower lip and mugs for the camera. This has been Rial's style for years. Back in the day, Iaia would bring the tea and cookies and lie down next to me in her patent leather platform shoes, which she never took off, not even in bed. My grandmother was the target audience for Rial's show, what's commonly known as "doña rosa," a housewife.
<v Jasmine Garsd>偶尔，如果有女人太性感，豪尔赫·里亚尔会咬住下唇对镜头做鬼脸。这是里亚尔多年的风格。过去，Iaia会拿来茶和饼干，穿着她那双漆皮厚底鞋躺在我旁边，她从不脱鞋，连睡觉都不脱。我的祖母正是里亚尔节目的目标受众，也就是通常所说的“doña rosa”，家庭主妇。

00:16:02.070 --> 00:16:34.870
<v Jasmine Garsd>She loved to hate the show, to look disapprovingly at the women and comment how much surgery she's had. "Una prostituta," a prostitute. "una loca." And they give her expensive gifts-- cars, vacations. And I'd look around me my grandmother's lonely apartment and think to myself, wow. That sounds pretty amazing. I knew I didn't want to be a doña rosa when I grew up.
<v Jasmine Garsd>她喜欢边看边讨厌这节目，不屑地看着那些女人并评论她们整了多少容。“妓女，”她说，“疯子。”节目给她们昂贵的礼物——车、假期。我环顾祖母孤独的公寓，心里想，哇。那听起来真棒。我知道长大后我不想成为“doña rosa”。

00:16:34.870 --> 00:17:00.750
<v Jasmine Garsd>When I was a teenager, I moved to the US and eventually became a journalist. I've lived here for 15 years. Sometimes when I get homesick, I stream Intrusos on YouTube. I leave it on when I cook and clean. When I watch it, I'm not 5,000 miles away. Iaia is alive, nothing has changed much. Nothing ever changes on Argentine daytime TV. Until suddenly, a few months ago, it did.
<v Jasmine Garsd>十几岁时我搬到了美国，最终成为了一名记者。我已经在这儿住了15年。有时想家了，我会在YouTube上流媒体看《Intrusos》。做饭和打扫时我都会开着。看它的时候，我不觉得身处5000英里外。Iaia还活着，什么都没变多少。阿根廷日间电视节目从不改变。直到几个月前，突然变了。

00:17:00.750 --> 00:17:08.070
<v Jasmine Garsd>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Jasmine Garsd>[音乐播放]

00:17:08.070 --> 00:17:12.350
<v Man>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Man>[西班牙语对话]

00:17:12.350 --> 00:17:30.180
<v Jasmine Garsd>One night in February, I was at home in New York cleaning my kitchen. Intrusos was on in the background and I heard this woman with a raspy Lauren Bacall voice. I turned around, soapy sponge in hand, and squinted at the screen-- a tattooed, heavyset woman wearing sneakers.
<v Jasmine Garsd>二月的一天晚上，我在纽约家里清理厨房。《Intrusos》在后台播放，我听到一个有着劳伦·白考尔般沙哑嗓音的女人的声音。我拿着海绵转过身，眯眼看着屏幕——一个纹身、身材魁梧、穿着运动鞋的女人。

00:17:30.180 --> 00:17:45.590
<v Jasmine Garsd>I recognized this woman, a comedian named Senorita Bimbo. The stage name, Bimbo, is ironic. She's anything but. In fact, the very next thing she did was look directly into the camera and offer a statistic about illegal abortions.
<v Jasmine Garsd>我认出了她，一个叫Senorita Bimbo的喜剧演员。“Bimbo”（花瓶）这个艺名很讽刺。她绝非如此。事实上，她做的下一件事就是直视镜头，提供了一个关于非法堕胎的统计数据。

00:17:45.590 --> 00:17:50.290
<v Senorita Bimbo>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Senorita Bimbo>[西班牙语对话]

00:17:50.290 --> 00:18:09.780
<v Jasmine Garsd>"500,000 women in Argentina have illegal abortions every year," she said. She was wearing a bright green handkerchief around her neck, a provocative symbol everyone in Argentina knows-- the symbol of the fight to legalize abortion. For years, activists have been pushing to get Congress to vote on it.
<v Jasmine Garsd>“阿根廷每年有50万名女性进行非法堕胎，”她说。她的脖子上围着一条鲜艳的绿手帕，这是阿根廷每个人都知道的挑衅性标志——象征着堕胎合法化的斗争。多年来，活动家们一直在推动国会对此进行投票。

00:18:09.780 --> 00:18:32.350
<v Jasmine Garsd>When I was growing up, abortion was something you just didn't talk about in Argentina, a Catholic country. It's still not something that comes up on daytime TV. Reproductive rights? That's just not Intrusos material. Though here was Jorge Rial, the host, looking intently at Senorita Bimbo.
<v Jasmine Garsd>我成长过程中，在阿根廷这个天主教国家，堕胎是不被谈论的话题。它至今也不是日间电视节目的素材。生殖权利？那根本不是《Intrusos》的内容。但豪尔赫·里亚尔这位主持人正盯着Senorita Bimbo。

00:18:32.350 --> 00:18:56.440
<v Jasmine Garsd>A few hours later, one of my best friends texted me, "Did you watch Intrusos today?" I sat down at my laptop and started scrolling through the descriptions of the last few episodes. The guests were names I knew-- academics, writers, comedians. What they had in common was, they were all feminists, people who have been on the fringes for years, criticizing sexism in Argentina and demanding women's rights.
<v Jasmine Garsd>几个小时后，我最好的朋友给我发短信：“你今天看《Intrusos》了吗？”我坐在笔记本电脑前，开始滚动浏览过去几集的描述。嘉宾名单里有我熟悉的名字——学者、作家、喜剧演员。他们的共同点是都是女权主义者，都是多年来边缘化的人物，批评阿根廷的性别歧视并要求女性权利。

00:18:56.440 --> 00:19:17.430
<v Jasmine Garsd>I started binge watching. In each episode, there was a nuanced conversation about feminism. Rial looked kind of meek, but not in his usual, "I've been overpowered by sexy ladies" way. He kept delivering these really impassioned monologues saying, I don't want to be a misogynist, a "machista." I'm a recovering machista.
<v Jasmine Garsd>我开始狂看。每一集中，都有关于女权主义的微妙对话。里亚尔看起来很温顺，但不是那种通常的“被性感女士压倒”的样子。他一直发表充满激情的独白，说：“我不想成为一个厌女者，一个‘大男子主义者’。我是一个正在康复中的大男子主义者。”

00:19:17.430 --> 00:19:23.780
<v Jorge Rial>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Jorge Rial>[西班牙语对话]

00:19:23.780 --> 00:19:45.640
<v Jasmine Garsd>The Argentine everyman now appeared to be an earnest feminist. This was not the Rial I grew up with. This was not the TV I grew up with. What happened? Could this possibly be sincere? I flew to Argentina to find out.
<v Jasmine Garsd>这位阿根廷平民偶像现在看起来是个真诚的女权主义者。这不是我长大的那个里亚尔。这不是我长大的那种电视。发生了什么？这可能是真心的吗？我飞往阿根廷一探究竟。

00:19:45.640 --> 00:19:53.430
<v Jasmine Garsd>As soon as I got there, I went to the studios where Intrusos is taped. I met Ana Laura Guevara, one of the show's executive producers.
<v Jasmine Garsd>一到那，我就去了《Intrusos》的录制工作室。我见到了执行制片人之一，安娜·劳拉·格瓦拉。

00:19:53.430 --> 00:19:54.900
<v Ana Laura Guevara>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Ana Laura Guevara>[西班牙语对话]

00:19:54.900 --> 00:20:01.080
<v Interpreter>Being live involves a lot of adrenalin. I really, really love the adrenalin.
<v Interpreter>现场直播需要大量的肾上腺素。我真的、真的很爱那种肾上腺素。

00:20:01.080 --> 00:20:23.760
<v Jasmine Garsd>To be honest, I wasn't expecting an Intrusos executive producer to be a woman, especially not one like Ana Laura, a self-proclaimed feminist. I had a really hard time wrapping my head around the fact that, for 18 years, she had been behind this totally trashy and objectifying show. Ana Laura told me, it's just a job, one she's good at. It's intensely competitive.
<v Jasmine Garsd>老实说，我没料到《Intrusos》的执行制片人会是个女人，尤其不是像安娜·劳拉这样自称女权主义者的人。我很难接受一个事实，那就是在过去的18年里，她一直在这个完全低俗且物化女性的节目背后操盘。安娜·劳拉说，这只是一份工作，一份她擅长的工作。而且竞争极其激烈。

