<v Jasmine Garsd>It dipped to 3.4.
<v Jasmine Garsd>降到了3.4。

00:21:11.050 --> 00:21:26.780
<v Ira Glass>The ratings plummet. Nobody cares. Ana Laura orders them to end the segment early. Interest lags for an instant, and Intrusos moves on.
<v Ira Glass>收视率暴跌。没人关心。安娜·劳拉命令他们提前结束片段。兴趣瞬间消失，《Intrusos》继续播放下一个内容。

00:21:26.780 --> 00:21:52.700
<v Ira Glass>The story of how the feminists intruded into Intrusos is its own soap opera. There are a gazillion gossip shows in Argentina. It's like this whole universe. Back in January, one of the shows interviewed this famous singer, a leathery guy in a tropical shirt. In the middle of the interview, the singer casually repeats this awful saying I used to hear as a kid-- if someone wants to rape you, relax and enjoy it.
<v Ira Glass>女权主义者如何入侵《Intrusos》的故事本身就是一出肥皂剧。阿根廷有成千上万的八卦节目。这是一个完整的宇宙。回到一月，其中一个节目采访了一位著名的歌手，一个穿着热带衬衫、皮肤粗糙的男人。在采访中，歌手随意地重复了我小时候常听到的那句糟糕的谚语——如果有人想强奸你，放松享受吧。

00:21:52.700 --> 00:21:57.080
<v Man>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Man>[西班牙语对话]

00:21:57.080 --> 00:22:07.290
<v Jasmine Garsd>The first time I heard that was when I was nine years old. I was in the locker room and a girl blurted it out. I thought it was advice. A lot of my friends did, too.
<v Jasmine Garsd>我第一次听到那是九岁时。我在更衣室里，一个女孩冲口而出。我以为那是建议。我的很多朋友也这么以为。

00:22:07.290 --> 00:22:16.870
<v Jasmine Garsd>So the singer says this offensive thing. A few days later, on another talk show, a soap opera star blows up about it. Her name is Araceli Gonzales.
<v Jasmine Garsd>所以歌手说了那句冒犯的话。几天后，在另一个谈话节目里，一位肥皂剧明星对此爆发了。她的名字叫阿拉塞利·冈萨雷斯。

00:22:16.870 --> 00:22:30.010
<v Jasmine Garsd>When I was a kid, her soap opera was huge. She played a mute. A hunk with feathered hair would talk at her while she listened tearfully. But now she wasn't mute. She said the singer's remark made her sick.
<v Jasmine Garsd>小时候，她的肥皂剧火得不得了。她扮演一个哑巴。一个留着羽毛发型、肌肉发达的男人对着她说话，而她含泪倾听。但现在她不再沉默。她说歌手的评论让她恶心。

00:22:30.010 --> 00:22:33.910
<v Araceli Gonzales>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Araceli Gonzales>[西班牙语对话]

00:22:33.910 --> 00:22:49.340
<v Jasmine Garsd>It was kind of beautiful seeing her get angry after so many years of playing a character literally defined by silence. Ana Laura, from Intrusos, saw the fight happening on TV and she wanted to get a piece of it. She booked Araceli to come on the show.
<v Jasmine Garsd>在多年扮演一个以沉默为定义的角色后，看到她发火真是一种美感。《Intrusos》的安娜·劳拉看到了电视上的这场争吵，她想分一杯羹。她预约了阿拉塞利来节目。

00:22:49.340 --> 00:22:53.260
<v Man>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Man>[西班牙语对话]

00:22:53.260 --> 00:23:13.060
<v Man>It was a typical day on Intrusos. Jorge Rial talked about how much granny panties used to turn him on as a kid. Two former showgirls argued. And then it was Araceli's turn. And just because Araceli had gotten mad about the rape comment, one of the panelists introduces her as a feminist. As soon as Araceli got a chance, she corrected him.
<v Man>那是《Intrusos》典型的一天。豪尔赫·里亚尔谈论小时候奶奶的内裤是如何让他兴奋的。两位前舞女在争吵。然后轮到阿拉塞利了。就因为阿拉塞利对强奸评论发了火，其中一位小组成员介绍她为女权主义者。阿拉塞利一有机会，就纠正了他。

00:23:13.060 --> 00:23:16.900
<v Araceli Gonzales>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Araceli Gonzales>[西班牙语对话]

00:23:16.900 --> 00:23:24.670
<v Jasmine Garsd>She says, "I heard you refer to me as a feminist just now, and I am not a feminist." She is vehemently wagging her finger as she says this.
<v Jasmine Garsd>她说：“我刚听到你称我为女权主义者，我不是女权主义者。”她在说这话时激烈地摇着手指。

00:23:24.670 --> 00:23:29.750
<v Araceli Gonzales>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Araceli Gonzales>[西班牙语对话]

00:23:29.750 --> 00:23:39.990
<v Jasmine Garsd>"I have a wonderful husband and a lovely son whom I love very much, and I respect men." This set off another firestorm. Here's Ana Laura.
<v Jasmine Garsd>“我有一个很棒的丈夫和一个我深爱的可爱儿子，我尊重男人。”这又引发了一场风暴。这是安娜·劳拉说的。

00:23:39.990 --> 00:23:43.240
<v Ana Laura>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Ana Laura>[西班牙语对话]

00:23:43.240 --> 00:23:54.220
<v Interpreter>So people started tweeting about it. And we saw that feminists started to respond. So everything exploded.
<v Interpreter>于是人们开始在推特上讨论。我们看到女权主义者开始回应。于是炸了锅了。

00:23:54.220 --> 00:24:08.580
<v Jasmine Garsd>There were the kinds of tweets you would expect like, quote, "What the fuck does loving your husband and son have to do with being a feminist, you moron?" And here it was, feminists versus the soap opera star, a fight made for daytime television.
<v Jasmine Garsd>推文全是意料之中的那种，比如，“爱你的丈夫和儿子和你是不是女权主义者有什么关系，你这个白痴？”这就是女权主义者对阵肥皂剧明星，一场为日间电视而生的争斗。

00:24:08.580 --> 00:24:17.760
<v Jasmine Garsd>And Ana Laura knew it. And she also knew Jorge Rial, the host of the show-- something had been changing with him lately. Like, he'd been saying to anyone who would listen--
<v Jasmine Garsd>安娜·劳拉知道这一点。她也了解《Intrusos》的主持人豪尔赫·里亚尔——他最近有些改变。就像他一直在对任何人说——

00:24:17.760 --> 00:24:19.960
<v Ana Laura>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Ana Laura>[西班牙语对话]

00:24:19.960 --> 00:24:24.690
<v Interpreter>I am a machista in recovery. I'm trying to find myself.
<v Interpreter>我是一个正在康复中的大男子主义者。我正在寻找自我。

00:24:24.690 --> 00:24:31.840
<v Jasmine Garsd>So she approached him in the dressing room and they started talking. Maybe we should have a feminist on the show to explain what feminism is.
<v Jasmine Garsd>所以她在化妆间找到了他，他们开始交谈。也许我们应该请一位女权主义者上节目解释什么是女权主义。

00:24:31.840 --> 00:24:34.300
<v Ana Laura>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Ana Laura>[西班牙语对话]

00:24:34.300 --> 00:24:41.460
<v Interpreter>We hadn't discussed that beforehand. But this day on the dressing room, I think that he was really into it.
<v Interpreter>我们之前没讨论过。但在化妆间的那天，我想他真的很感兴趣。

00:24:41.460 --> 00:24:57.410
<v Jasmine Garsd>They decided on a well-known feminist academic, Flor Freijo. And even she'll tell you she's a safe bet for a show like Intrusos. She's thin and blonde. So Flor gets invited to Intrusos and the very first question Jorge Rial asks her is, what is feminism?
<v Jasmine Garsd>他们定了一位著名的女权主义学者，弗洛尔·弗雷霍。即便她自己也会告诉你，对于《Intrusos》这样的节目来说，她是稳妥的选择。她瘦削、金发。所以弗洛尔受邀来到《Intrusos》，豪尔赫·里亚尔问她的第一个问题就是：什么是女权主义？

00:24:57.410 --> 00:25:01.420
<v Jorge Rial>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Jorge Rial>[西班牙语对话]

00:25:01.420 --> 00:25:04.830
<v Flor Freijo>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Flor Freijo>[西班牙语对话]

00:25:04.830 --> 00:25:15.250
<v Flor Freijo>I didn't prepare anything. I didn't prepare a speech. I didn't have time. So I went open to listen to the questions and explain things just as I do to my students in a class.
<v Flor Freijo>我没准备任何东西。我没准备演讲稿。我没时间。所以我开放地去听那些问题，就像我在课堂上给学生解释一样。

00:25:15.250 --> 00:25:17.850
<v Flor Freijo>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Flor Freijo>[西班牙语对话]

00:25:17.850 --> 00:26:04.780
<v Jasmine Garsd>Of all the strange things I'd seen on Argentine TV, this might be one of the oddest. Against a neon fizz background, Flor Freijo does a feminism 101. At the bottom of the screen, a banner in bold letters reads, "Feminism-- it's a movement for women's rights." Flor starts explaining, feminism is a movement for women's rights. It started in the 19th century. It has to do with the division of labor, child rearing. Jorge Rial is listening, completely mesmerized, his little eyebrows furrowed, scratching his beard. And while all this is happening, Ana Laura is sitting in the control room upstairs, watching everything, of course-- and also keeping her eye on the ratings monitor. The control room is usually a chaotic mess of yelling, but now, with Flor speaking--
<v Jasmine Garsd>在阿根廷电视上我见过的所有奇怪的事情中，这可能是最奇怪的之一。在霓虹灯背景前，弗洛尔·弗雷霍进行了一次女权主义入门讲座。屏幕下方，一个加粗的横幅写着：“女权主义——这是一场争取妇女权利的运动。”弗洛尔开始解释，女权主义是争取妇女权利的运动。它始于19世纪。它与劳动分工、育儿有关。豪尔赫·里亚尔听着，完全入迷了，眉毛皱起，抓着胡子。而当这一切发生时，安娜·劳拉正坐在楼上的控制室里，当然也在观察这一切——同时也盯着收视率监测器。控制室通常是一片嘈杂混乱，但现在，弗洛尔说话时——

00:26:04.780 --> 00:26:08.310
<v Ana Laura>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Ana Laura>[西班牙语对话]

00:26:08.310 --> 00:26:23.820
<v Interpreter>And when we were watching her talking, Flor, the control room went quiet. We were all paying attention to what she was saying. But we were all quiet. We were really like, silently watching and learning from her.
<v Interpreter>当我们看着弗洛尔谈话时，控制室安静了下来。我们都在注意听她说的内容。我们都很安静。我们真的像是在默默地学习。

00:26:23.820 --> 00:26:40.770
<v Jasmine Garsd>And then the spell is broken because the phone rings in the control room. It's Araceli, the soap opera star who's the whole reason Flora's here. She wants to talk to Flor live, right now. Everyone in the control room is geared up for a good old-fashioed Intrusos spat.
<v Jasmine Garsd>然后咒语被打破了，因为控制室电话响了。是阿拉塞利，那个让弗洛尔来到这里的肥皂剧明星。她现在就要现场和弗洛尔通话。控制室里的每个人都准备好迎接一场老式《Intrusos》风格的争吵。

00:26:40.770 --> 00:26:44.380
<v Araceli Gonzales>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Araceli Gonzales>[西班牙语对话]

00:26:44.380 --> 00:26:45.670
<v Jasmine Garsd>Flor was kind of shocked.
<v Jasmine Garsd>弗洛尔有点震惊。

00:26:45.670 --> 00:26:48.070
<v Flor Freijo>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Flor Freijo>[西班牙语对话]

00:26:48.070 --> 00:26:54.030
<v Flor Freijo>I didn't know that Araceli was going to call. I had no idea of what was going to happen.
<v Flor Freijo>我不知道阿拉塞利会打电话。我不知道会发生什么。

00:26:54.030 --> 00:27:13.050
<v Jasmine Garsd>But it wasn't an ambush. Araceli wasn't calling to fight. Instead she tells Flor, I've been listening really well to what you're saying. And she wanted the audience to know that she didn't know what feminism was until just now, when she was watching TV and saw Flor explain it.
<v Jasmine Garsd>但这并非埋伏。阿拉塞利打电话不是为了吵架。相反，她告诉弗洛尔：“我一直在认真听你说的。我想让观众知道，我刚才看电视看到弗洛尔解释之前，根本不知道女权主义是什么。”

00:27:13.050 --> 00:27:33.410
<v Jasmine Garsd>She starts telling the story of her life through various generations of women, her own single mother and herself. She talks about how she had been sexually abused as a child and emotionally abused as an adult. And Araceli told Flor, I know what you're talking about and I agree with you. If this means being a feminist, then I'm a feminist.
<v Jasmine Garsd>她开始讲述自己通过几代女性——她单身的母亲和她自己——的生活故事。她谈到童年时曾被性虐待，成年后被情感虐待。阿拉塞利告诉弗洛尔：“我知道你在谈论什么，我同意你。如果这就是成为女权主义者的含义，那我是女权主义者。”

00:27:33.410 --> 00:27:36.710
<v Araceli Gonzales>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Araceli Gonzales>[西班牙语对话]

00:27:36.710 --> 00:27:56.430
<v Jasmine Garsd>Flor nods, and gives a thumbs up. By the way, this is never how Intrusos finishes. People don't just listen to each other and change their minds. And the ratings-- Ana Laura says the ratings were great, strong enough that she decided, let's do this again tomorrow. And so it began.
<v Jasmine Garsd>弗洛尔点头，竖起大拇指。顺便说一句，这可不是《Intrusos》典型的结束方式。人们不会只是听对方说话然后改变主意。至于收视率——安娜·劳拉说收视率很棒，好到她决定：“明天再做一次。”于是它开始了。

00:27:56.430 --> 00:27:59.950
<v Jasmine Garsd>[OMINOUS MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Jasmine Garsd>[不祥的音乐播放]

00:27:59.950 --> 00:28:31.860
<v Jasmine Garsd>Over the next few days, some of the most famous feminists in Argentina came onto Intrusos. Comedians, authors, professors-- audiences were stunned. Someone tweeted, "My ideology is starting to converge with Jorge Rial's, and that terrifies me." It was pretty strange for everyone. This very misogynistic show had suddenly become like, the public town hall on feminism in Argentina. And the ratings were not just good. Ana Laura says they were higher than normal. She was delighted that she could keep this going.
<v Jasmine Garsd>接下来的几天，阿根廷一些最著名的女权主义者来到《Intrusos》。喜剧演员、作家、教授——观众们惊呆了。有人发推特：“我的意识形态开始与豪尔赫·里亚尔趋同，这让我很害怕。”这对每个人来说都很奇怪。这个非常厌女的节目突然变成了阿根廷关于女权主义的公共市政厅。收视率不仅很好。安娜·劳拉说比平时更高。她很高兴能继续这样做。

00:28:31.860 --> 00:28:33.660
<v Ana Laura>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Ana Laura>[西班牙语对话]

00:28:33.660 --> 00:28:51.720
<v Interpreter>There's a journalist called Luciana Baker. She is also very important in feminism and she's an old friend from college. And she came to our show. And when we met backstage we were like, not even in our wildest dreams we could have dreamt about this, you being here in this type of show.
<v Interpreter>有个叫卢西亚娜·贝克的记者。她在女权主义中也非常重要，她是我的老同学。她来到我们的节目。我们在后台见面时说，我们甚至在最疯狂的梦里也想不到，你会来到这样的节目。

00:28:51.720 --> 00:29:09.900
<v Jasmine Garsd>Midway through all of this is when I tuned in, when I started streaming in New York. The show was like, going through hundreds of years of feminism in a couple of days. They passed through topics like LGBT rights, workplace harassment, income inequality. And then the most taboo thing of all-- abortion.
<v Jasmine Garsd>这一切进行到一半时我收看了节目，我在纽约开始流媒体收看。节目就像是在几天内经历了数百年的女权主义历史。他们谈论了LGBT权利、职场骚扰、收入不平等。然后是所有禁忌中最禁忌的话题——堕胎。

00:29:09.900 --> 00:29:30.000
<v Jasmine Garsd>Jorge Rial tied the green handkerchief around his wrist, the one activists who want to legalize abortion wear. And then he invited the large woman with the gravelly voice who I saw at home in New York City, Senorita Bimbo. Right off the bat she said, the fact that there's a fat girl on Argentine TV is already a victory.
<v Jasmine Garsd>豪尔赫·里亚尔在手腕上系了绿色手帕，那是想要堕胎合法化的活动家戴的。然后他邀请了我在纽约家里看到的那个声音沙哑的高大女性，Senorita Bimbo。她上来就说，阿根廷电视上有个胖女孩本身就是一场胜利。

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:32.790
<v Senorita Bimbo>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Senorita Bimbo>[西班牙语对话]

00:29:32.790 --> 00:29:35.040
<v Jasmine Garsd>She told me she was actually pretty nervous.
<v Jasmine Garsd>她告诉我其实当时很紧张。

00:29:35.040 --> 00:29:36.450
<v Senorita Bimbo>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Senorita Bimbo>[西班牙语对话]

00:29:36.450 --> 00:29:44.820
<v Interpreter>The first thing I thought was, what they're all going to say is like, what is this fat girl doing here, this fat girl feminazi?
<v Interpreter>我脑子里的第一个念头是，他们都会说：“这个胖女孩在这里干什么，这个胖女孩女权纳粹？”

00:29:44.820 --> 00:29:47.060
<v Jasmine Garsd>But she powered through. She had a mission.
<v Jasmine Garsd>但她坚持下来了。她有使命。

00:29:47.060 --> 00:29:48.970
<v Senorita Bimbo>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Senorita Bimbo>[西班牙语对话]

00:29:48.970 --> 00:30:15.570
<v Interpreter>I knew I wanted to talk about abortion. My plan was to at least mention it. And I just sat down and started talking. I felt like I was going to battle, where I had to use words as arrows, because abortion is something that you don't say. It's something that you talk about in hushed tones if you have one.
<v Interpreter>我知道我想谈论堕胎。我的计划是至少提到它。我只是坐下来开始谈。感觉就像要上战场，我必须用言语作为箭，因为堕胎是不能说的事情。如果你有这种经历，那是只能在耳边低语的事情。

00:30:15.570 --> 00:30:29.480
<v Jasmine Garsd>On Intrusos, Senorita Bimbo talked about how abortion is so taboo, you don't even talk about it in fiction. In Argentine TV and film, unwanted pregnancy is solved by a villain pushing you down the stairs and causing you to miscarry.
<v Jasmine Garsd>在《Intrusos》上，Senorita Bimbo谈到堕胎是多么禁忌，甚至在小说里都不敢提。在阿根廷的电视和电影里，意外怀孕的解决方法是一个反派把你推下楼梯导致流产。

00:30:29.480 --> 00:30:34.920
<v Senorita Bimbo>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Senorita Bimbo>[西班牙语对话]

00:30:34.920 --> 00:30:44.210
<v Jasmine Garsd>And then about 30 seconds before they cut to commercial and moved to the next guest, Senorita Bimbo said something about abortion that surprised even her.
<v Jasmine Garsd>然后在大约广告前30秒，转移到下一个嘉宾前，Senorita Bimbo说了关于堕胎的一句话，连她自己都惊讶了。

00:30:44.210 --> 00:30:46.000
<v Senorita Bimbo>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Senorita Bimbo>[西班牙语对话]

00:30:46.000 --> 00:31:30.060
<v Jasmine Garsd>Misoprostol-- she says, I want girls to know about misoprostol. This is a really big deal. Officially, misoprostol is a drug used to treat stomach ulcers, but it can also be used to induce labor. So in a continent where abortion is mostly banned, women take it if they want to miscarry. People call it the DIY abortion. She's talking about doing something illegal on one of the most popular daytime talk shows, watched by housewives. That same day, misoprostol was one of the most Googled words in the country. "I think you're underestimating your audience," Senorita Bimbo said on the show. Doña rosa is dead-- doña rosa, that stereotypical Argentine housewife.
<v Jasmine Garsd>“米索前列醇，”她说，“我想让女孩们知道米索前列醇。”这是一件大事。正式来说，米索前列醇是一种治疗胃溃疡的药物，但它也可以用来诱导分娩。所以在堕胎大多被禁止的大洲，女性如果想流产会吃它。人们称之为DIY堕胎。她在收视率最高的日间谈话节目之一，在家庭主妇们的注视下，谈论做违法的事情。就在那天，米索前列醇成了全国搜索次数最多的词。“我想你们低估了自己的观众，”Senorita Bimbo在节目上说。“Doña rosa”死了——那个典型的阿根廷家庭主妇。

00:31:30.060 --> 00:31:32.230
<v Senorita Bimbo>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Senorita Bimbo>[西班牙语对话]

00:31:32.230 --> 00:31:46.260
<v Interpreter>So the woman that is in front of the TV, and who needs her world to be explained to her through daytime TV, she just doesn't exist anymore.
<v Interpreter>所以那个坐在电视前，需要通过日间电视来解释世界的女人，她根本不存在了。

00:31:46.260 --> 00:32:08.110
<v Jasmine Garsd>Of course, none of this would have happened on Intrusos if the ratings had been bad. And the ratings were great for reasons that Jorge Rial and Ana Laura can claim no credit for at all. Feminism has been gaining critical mass in Argentina for the last couple of years. The movement was triggered by these brutal murders of young women, often by boyfriends, husbands, and fathers.
<v Jasmine Garsd>当然，如果收视率很差，这一切都不会在《Intrusos》上发生。收视率之所以好，豪尔赫·里亚尔和安娜·劳拉完全无法居功。在过去几年里，女权主义在阿根廷一直在积累临界质量。这场运动是由年轻女性被男友、丈夫和父亲残忍谋杀所触发的。

00:32:08.110 --> 00:32:24.170
<v Jasmine Garsd>Women started protesting. A whole crusade was born. It was called Ni Una Menos, not one less woman. And since 2015, this has grown to the point where it's impossible to ignore and has expanded to abortion rights, street harassment, and equal pay.
<v Jasmine Garsd>女性开始抗议。一场完整的运动诞生了。它叫“Ni Una Menos”，意为“少一个女性也不行”。自2015年以来，它已增长到无法忽视的地步，并扩展到堕胎权、街头骚扰和平等薪酬。

00:32:24.170 --> 00:32:40.590
<v Jasmine Garsd>It's young people on social media, comedians on YouTube, pop stars on Instagram, gigantic demonstrations. It just wasn't a topic for daytime talk shows until Jorge, Ana Laura, and Intrusos.
<v Jasmine Garsd>它是社交媒体上的年轻人、YouTube上的喜剧演员、Instagram上的流行歌星、巨大的示威活动。直到豪尔赫、安娜·劳拉和《Intrusos》，这在日间谈话节目里一直不是话题。

00:32:40.590 --> 00:33:08.240
<v Jasmine Garsd>During that week on Intrusos, there was this explosion of tweets from young girls perplexed but ecstatic to see feminism on daytime TV. This one girl, Anita Ocampo tweeted, "I showed my dad the Intrusos episode with Senorita Bimbo." I dropped by her house and she told me, these feminists were explaining to Jorge Rial all the things she'd tried to explain but couldn't get her parents to understand. So one night she approached her dad.
<v Jasmine Garsd>在那周《Intrusos》播出期间，社交媒体上涌现出一堆困惑但欣喜若狂的推文，那是年轻女孩们看到女权主义出现在日间电视上时的反应。有个女孩安妮塔·奥坎波发推：“我向我爸爸展示了Senorita Bimbo那集的《Intrusos》。”我去了她家，她说这些女权主义者正在向豪尔赫·里亚尔解释她尝试解释却无法让父母理解的所有事情。所以有一天晚上她去找爸爸。

00:33:08.240 --> 00:33:12.780
<v Anita Ocampo>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Anita Ocampo>[西班牙语对话]

00:33:12.780 --> 00:33:28.460
<v Jasmine Garsd>And she told him, if you watch this episode of Intrusos on YouTube with me, I'll massage your feet. She ended up getting the whole family to watch. She showed them Senorita Bimbo. She pointed to Jorge Rial wearing the same green handkerchief she wears and said, look, it's just like mine.
<v Jasmine Garsd>她告诉他，如果你和我一起在YouTube上看这集《Intrusos》，我就帮你按摩脚。她最终让全家都看了。她展示了Senorita Bimbo。她指着戴着和她一样绿色手帕的豪尔赫·里亚尔说：“看，这和我的一样。”

00:33:28.460 --> 00:33:31.890
<v Anita Ocampo>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Anita Ocampo>[西班牙语对话]

00:33:31.890 --> 00:33:43.260
<v Jasmine Garsd>It opened up a conversation, which she says they've been having ever since. Anita's mom says she saw Jorge Rial talk about how he's a recovering chauvinist and she says, so is she.
<v Jasmine Garsd>这开启了一段对话，她说他们从此一直在交流。安妮塔的妈妈说她看到豪尔赫·里亚尔谈论自己是一个康复中的大男子主义者，她说她也是。

00:33:43.260 --> 00:33:49.800
<v Anita's Mother>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Anita's Mother>[西班牙语对话]

00:33:49.800 --> 00:34:03.270
<v Jasmine Garsd>I'm at, like, 70% feminism, she says. I still have 30% left to go.
<v Jasmine Garsd>“我大概有70%的女权主义了，”她说，“还有30%要走。”

00:34:03.270 --> 00:34:39.940
<v Jasmine Garsd>During my week in Argentina I kept trying to talk to Jorge Rial, and he kept blowing me off. Had he really converted to feminism? Everyone I asked rolled their eyes and pointed to the last few decades of his career. They pointed to his recent vicious public fight with one of his daughters. They pointed to how late he is to the whole feminism thing. He's a Johnny come lately. He's only doing this because it will make him more popular. After days of giving me the runaround, he told me to just send my questions. And finally, on my very last night in Argentina, my phone lit up. It was voice memos from Jorge Rial.
<v Jasmine Garsd>在阿根廷的那周，我一直尝试和豪尔赫·里亚尔谈话，但他一直拒绝。他真的皈依了女权主义吗？我问过的每个人都翻白眼，并指着他过去几十年的职业生涯。他们指着他最近与女儿之一恶毒的公开争吵。他们指着他介入女权主义有多晚。他是个“迟来的约翰尼”。他做这些只是为了让自己更受欢迎。经过几天推诿，他让我把问题发过去。终于，在阿根廷的最后一晚，我的手机亮了。是豪尔赫·里亚尔的语音备忘录。

00:34:39.940 --> 00:34:44.010
<v Jorge Rial>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Jorge Rial>[西班牙语对话]

00:34:44.010 --> 00:34:49.860
<v Jasmine Garsd>"What happened to me?" Rial says. "What made me bring all these feminists onto Intrusos?"
<v Jasmine Garsd>“我发生了什么？”里亚尔说。“是什么让我把这些女权主义者带上《Intrusos》？”

00:34:49.860 --> 00:34:57.780
<v Jorge Rial>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Jorge Rial>[西班牙语对话]

00:34:57.780 --> 00:35:20.810
<v Jasmine Garsd>He talks about his 18-year-old daughter, Rosio, and how she's a feminist. "We have these very interesting talks over dinner," he says, "And she started opening this world up to me. I am 56 years old. I was raised in a completely sexist culture and I didn't get it. That's why I say I'm a recovering chauvinist, thanks to my daughter. My daughter made me change."
<v Jasmine Garsd>他谈到他18岁的女儿罗西奥，以及她是如何成为女权主义者的。“我们在晚餐时进行了一些非常有趣的对话，”他说，“她开始向我打开这个世界。我已经56岁了。我在完全性别歧视的文化中长大，我不懂这些。这就是为什么我说我是一个正在康复的大男子主义者，多亏了我女儿。我女儿让我改变了。”

00:35:20.810 --> 00:35:23.090
<v Jorge Rial>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Jorge Rial>[西班牙语对话]

00:35:23.090 --> 00:35:29.320
<v Jasmine Garsd>Jorge Rial knows that I think Intrusos is stupid. He knows most people do. That's the show's superpower.
<v Jasmine Garsd>豪尔赫·里亚尔知道我觉得《Intrusos》很愚蠢。他知道大多数人都这么觉得。那是节目的超能力。

00:35:29.320 --> 00:35:32.710
<v Jorge Rial>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Jorge Rial>[西班牙语对话]

00:35:32.710 --> 00:35:46.820
<v Jasmine Garsd>"Frívolo--" we're frivolous. We're a show about showbiz. No one suspected that this is where feminism could win. We eluded the firewalls that kept feminism off of TV. There was this wall, you couldn't talk about these things on TV.
<v Jasmine Garsd>“Frívolo——”我们很轻浮。我们是一个展示秀。没人怀疑这就是女权主义可以赢的地方。我们绕过了那些将女权主义挡在电视之外的防火墙。曾经有一堵墙，你在电视上不能谈论这些事情。

00:35:46.820 --> 00:35:57.150
<v Jasmine Garsd>And suddenly it happened on Intrusos. "But to be honest," he said, "it's all because of feminists. They knew any place is good, if you have a strong message."
<v Jasmine Garsd>突然它在《Intrusos》上发生了。“但说实话，”他说，“这全是因为女权主义者。只要有强有力的信息，她们知道任何地方都是好地方。”

00:35:57.150 --> 00:36:01.620
<v Jorge Rial>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Jorge Rial>[西班牙语对话]

00:36:01.620 --> 00:36:18.180
<v Jasmine Garsd>After the week of feminism, Intrusos was left with a split personality. These days, it's a mix of fighting starlets and women's rights activists. Jorge Rial's social media is a mix of World Cup woes, celebrity gossip, and then these really earnest feminist tweets.
<v Jasmine Garsd>在那一周的女权主义节目之后，《Intrusos》留下了一个分裂的人格。这些天，它是斗争中的明星和女权活动家的混合体。豪尔赫·里亚尔的社交媒体是世界杯的悲伤、名人八卦，然后是这些真正认真的女权主义推文的混合物。

00:36:18.180 --> 00:36:37.390
<v Jasmine Garsd>Like this one a few weeks ago-- "They came to make things better for the coming generations, for our daughters and their daughters, and also for men. The men who come after us must be better than us. We did everything wrong."
<v Jasmine Garsd>就像几周前的这条——“她们是为了让后代变得更好而来，为了我们的女儿和她们的女儿，也为了男人。我们之后的男人必须比我们更好。我们把一切都搞砸了。”

00:36:37.390 --> 00:37:01.980
<v Jasmine Garsd>The day after he tweeted that on June 14, Argentina's lower house of Congress approved a bill to legalize abortion. After an all-night debate, it barely passed by only four votes. And it yet has to pass the upper house. Still, outside Congress, thousands of women and activists who'd gathered to wait for the results celebrated wildly.
<v Jasmine Garsd>在他发出那条推文的次日，6月14日，阿根廷国会众议院批准了一项堕胎合法化法案。经过整夜辩论，仅以四票优势通过。它还必须通过参议院。尽管如此，在国会外，数千名聚集等待结果的女性和活动家欣喜若狂。

00:37:01.980 --> 00:37:22.470
<v Jasmine Garsd>Every time I spoke to those women about what role television like Intrusos played in all this, they got uncomfortable. On my last day in Argentina I grabbed a coffee with an old friend from high school, Jordana Timerman. She recently wrote an op-ed for the New York Times about the push to legalize abortion in Argentina, and we talked about the role pop culture played in that.
<v Jasmine Garsd>每当我与那些女性谈论《Intrusos》之类的电视在这一切中扮演的角色时，她们就会感到不安。在阿根廷的最后一天，我和高中老友乔丹娜·蒂默曼一起喝咖啡。她最近在《纽约时报》写了一篇关于阿根廷推动堕胎合法化的专栏，我们谈到了流行文化在其中的作用。

00:37:22.470 --> 00:37:35.470
<v Jordana Timerman>You need to have people like Rial, or pop culture, doña rosa, understanding that this is a necessary right. Because if not, it's not going to happen.
<v Jordana Timerman>你需要有像里亚尔这样的人，或者流行文化、doña rosa，理解这是一个必要的权利。因为如果不这样，它就不会发生。

00:37:35.470 --> 00:37:50.570
<v Jasmine Garsd>In other words, the message needs to go into homes in the most remote locations of the country. And TV is one of the only ways to do that. Jordana was saying Intrusos helped. But when I asked her about whether we should thank Jorge Rial, she just laughs.
<v Jasmine Garsd>换句话说，信息需要进入该国最偏远地区的家庭。电视是做到这一点的唯一途径。乔丹娜说《Intrusos》帮了大忙。但当我问她我们是否应该感谢豪尔赫·里亚尔时，她只是笑了。

00:37:50.570 --> 00:37:57.000
<v Jordana Timerman>I'm not going on record with that. Are you crazy? I have a name.
<v Jordana Timerman>我可不会录音说那个。你疯了吗？我还有名声呢。

00:37:57.000 --> 00:38:17.770
<v Jasmine Garsd>I know what she means. After so many years of awful television and this guy's shenanigans, I just don't want to tip my hat to him. And maybe that's part of his penance. He did something good and no one will ever thank him for it.
<v Jasmine Garsd>我知道她的意思。在多年的糟糕电视和这个人的恶作剧之后，我只是不想向他致敬。也许那是他苦修的一部分。他做了一件好事，但永远不会有人感谢他。

00:38:17.770 --> 00:38:33.280
<v Ira Glass>Jasmine Garsd, she's an NPR reporter. This story was co-produced with Marianne McCune as part of a collaboration with the NPR podcast, Rough Translation. If you have not heard that show, the premise is they look at how things we talk about here in the United States get talked about elsewhere around the world.
<v Ira Glass>洁丝敏·加尔德，她是NPR记者。这个故事是与玛丽安·麦昆联合制作的，作为与NPR播客《Rough Translation》合作的一部分。如果你没听过那个节目，其前提是看看我们在美国谈论的事情在世界其他地方是如何被谈论的。

00:38:33.280 --> 00:38:45.390
<v Ira Glass>This leads into all kinds of stories that take you deep into other cultures and countries in this way. This is very unusual. Rough Translation, find it at Apple podcasts or the nine million other places you can get a podcast these days.
<v Ira Glass>这导致了各种各样的故事，将你深入带入其他文化和国家。这非常罕见。《Rough Translation》，在Apple Podcasts或你现在可以获取播客的其他九百万个地方找到它。

00:38:45.390 --> 00:39:20.910
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "MACHISTA" BY BUFFALO MOON]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - “MACHISTA” BY BUFFALO MOON]

00:39:20.910 --> 00:39:39.300
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, OK, you've heard about good cop, bad cop. But what about a bad cop who now says he's a good cop, and really, really, really, really wants everybody to believe him? That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，好吧，你听说过好警察、坏警察。但如果有一个说自己现在是好警察的坏警察，而且非常、非常、非常、非常想让每个人都相信他，那会怎样？那是来自芝加哥公共广播电台，一分钟后节目继续。

00:39:39.300 --> 00:39:56.240
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's program, Change You Can Maybe Believe In. We have stories where things happen that you want to believe, you wish you could believe, but something tells you maybe you should not believe. We've arrived at Act Two of our program. Act Two, You Have The Right To Remain Angry.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目，关于你可以也许去相信的改变。我们有这样的故事：发生了你想相信、你希望你能相信的事情，但某种东西告诉你也许你不该相信。我们到了节目的第二幕。第二幕，你有权保持愤怒。

00:39:56.240 --> 00:40:09.470
<v Ira Glass>This story is about a man whose job was to uphold the law and he was terrible at it. He says he's changed though, completely changed. Lilly Sullivan, one of our producers, heard about him from some video she saw.
<v Ira Glass>这个故事是关于一个以执法为工作但却极其糟糕的人。但他声称自己改变了，彻底改变了。莉莉·沙利文，我们的一位制片人，从她看到的视频中听说了他。

00:40:09.470 --> 00:40:17.510
<v Lilly Sullivan>There's this news story. I've watched it a bunch of times, my friend sent it to me. Not because she thought I'd like it-- she knew I wouldn't.
<v Lilly Sullivan>有这么个新闻故事。我看了很多次，我朋友发给我的。不是因为她觉得我会喜欢——她知道我不会。

00:40:17.510 --> 00:40:29.470
<v Reporter>It all went down on this block in Benton Harbor, Michigan. Jameel McGee says he was minding his own business when a police officer accused him of, and arrested him for, dealing drugs.
<v Reporter>这一切发生在密歇根州本顿港的这个街区。贾米尔·麦基说他当时只是各干各的，一个警察指控他贩毒并将他逮捕。

00:40:29.470 --> 00:40:31.360
<v Reporter>Are you saying the officer made it up?
<v Reporter>你是说那个警察编造的？

00:40:31.360 --> 00:40:33.620
<v Jameel Mcgee>Yeah, it was all made up.
<v Jameel Mcgee>对，全是编造的。

00:40:33.620 --> 00:41:00.620
<v Lilly Sullivan>This was on CBS national news in 2016. The guy talking, who got framed, is black. The cop who framed him is white. So after it comes out that the guy had been wrongly convicted he gets out of prison. He'd been in for three years. And he gets this job at a coffee shop. You know who his boss is? The cop, the guy who framed him. He was now the manager there.
<v Lilly Sullivan>这是2016年CBS全国新闻。那个被诬陷、正在谈论的人是黑人。那个诬陷他的警察是白人。所以在那个男人被错误定罪的事实浮出水面后，他出狱了。他在里面待了三年。然后他在咖啡店得到了一份工作。你知道他的老板是谁吗？那个警察，诬陷他的那个人。他现在是那里的经理。

00:41:00.620 --> 00:41:05.810
<v Reporter>By sheer coincidence, they now work side by side in the same cafe.
<v Reporter>纯属巧合，他们现在在那家咖啡馆并肩工作。

00:41:05.810 --> 00:41:07.190
<v Andrew Collins>Excuse me?
<v Andrew Collins>抱歉？

00:41:07.190 --> 00:41:19.460
<v Reporter>And it was in these cramped quarters that the bad cop and the wrongfully accused had no choice but to have it out.
<v Reporter>正是在这种狭窄的空间里，坏警察和被诬陷者别无选择，只能摊牌。

00:41:19.460 --> 00:41:23.390
<v Lilly Sullivan>The cop apologized, and the guy who was framed forgave him.
<v Lilly Sullivan>警察道歉了，被诬陷的那个人原谅了他。

00:41:23.390 --> 00:41:26.210
<v Jameel Mcgee>That was pretty much what I needed to hear.
<v Jameel Mcgee>那差不多是我需要听到的全部了。

00:41:26.210 --> 00:41:29.980
<v Reporter>Today, they're not only cordial, they're friends.
<v Reporter>今天，他们不仅友好，而且是朋友。

00:41:29.980 --> 00:41:33.140
<v Lilly Sullivan>The reporter couldn't stop fawning over how lovely it was.
<v Lilly Sullivan>记者滔滔不绝地吹捧这是多么美好。

00:41:33.140 --> 00:41:41.960
<v Reporter>Such close friends, not long ago, Jameel actually told Andrew he loved him.
<v Reporter>如此亲密的朋友，不久前，贾米尔甚至告诉安德鲁他爱他。

00:41:41.960 --> 00:42:03.050
<v Lilly Sullivan>The problem with this story was, it wasn't the whole story. When my friend sent it to me, she told me the cop, whose name is Andrew Collins, he didn't just put one innocent guy in prison. I Googled this cop and it turns out he falsified police reports and warrants. He lied on the stand. At least 62 cases were either overturned or got thrown out.
<v Lilly Sullivan>这个故事的问题在于，它不是全部的故事。我朋友发给我时，她告诉我那个叫安德鲁·柯林斯的警察，他不仅仅把一个无辜者关进监狱。我用谷歌搜索了这个警察，结果发现他伪造警方报告和搜查令。他在证人席上撒谎。至少62起案件被推翻或撤销。

00:42:03.050 --> 00:42:25.340
<v Lilly Sullivan>The small town of Benton Harbor ended up having to pay five and a half million dollars in settlements for civil rights violations. Then he befriends one guy and all that goes away? None of the other 61 victims even gets a mention? And then he gets to go on TV and tell his story over and over, because annoyingly, America seems to love this kind of story of forgiveness.
<v Lilly Sullivan>本顿港这个小镇最终不得不支付550万美元的和解金，因为侵犯民权。然后他与一个人成为朋友，这一切就消失了？其他61名受害者甚至连名字都没提？然后他能去电视上不断地讲述他的故事，因为令人恼火的是，美国似乎热衷于这种宽恕的故事。

00:42:25.340 --> 00:42:29.890
<v Steve Harvey>This involves my next guest, Jameel, and former police officer, Andrew.
<v Steve Harvey>这涉及到我的下一位嘉宾贾米尔，以及前警察安德鲁。

00:42:29.890 --> 00:42:33.680
<v Lilly Sullivan>That's talk show host, Steve Harvey. And here's Megyn Kelly on NBC.
<v Lilly Sullivan>那是脱口秀主持人史蒂夫·哈维。还有NBC的梅根·凯利。

00:42:33.680 --> 00:42:42.170
<v Megyn Kelly>Now our series, "Hopeful Holidays," and the story of two men who prove there is always hope for forgiveness and redemption.
<v Megyn Kelly>现在是我们的系列报道“希望假期”，两个证明宽恕和救赎永远有希望的男人的故事。

00:42:42.170 --> 00:42:43.610
<v Lilly Sullivan>Mike Huckabee on Fox.
<v Lilly Sullivan>福克斯的迈克·赫卡比。

00:42:43.610 --> 00:42:53.370
<v Mike Huckabee>I don't know of anything more powerful to help us through not only forgiveness and grace, but also racial reconciliation. And God bless you both.
<v Mike Huckabee>除了宽恕和恩典，以及种族和解，我不认为有什么比这更有力量能帮助我们了。愿上帝保佑你们两个。

00:42:53.370 --> 00:42:55.790
<v Mike Huckabee>[APPLAUSE]
<v Mike Huckabee>[掌声]

00:42:55.790 --> 00:43:11.300
<v Lilly Sullivan>Racial reconciliation-- really? That's pretty big, as if this one story could hit Undo on a whole country's worth of harm. These stories are always about people who forgive, preaching this forgiveness thing as some kind of tidy remedy that can set the world right.
<v Lilly Sullivan>种族和解——真的吗？那可真是了不起，就好像这一个故事就能撤销一个国家所带来的所有伤害一样。这些故事总是关于那些宽恕他人的人，鼓吹宽恕是一种可以矫正世界的灵丹妙药。

00:43:11.300 --> 00:43:22.460
<v Reporter>And clearly, if these two guys in the coffee shop can set aside their bitter grounds, what's our excuse?
<v Reporter>显然，如果咖啡馆里的这两个人都能放下恩怨，我们的借口是什么？

00:43:22.460 --> 00:43:35.430
<v Lilly Sullivan>Look, I'm not from this town. This had nothing to do with me. But I wondered if lots of people there saw this coverage and rolled their eyes. What about the people who don't forgive? Visit Benton Harbor. They're not hard to find.
<v Lilly Sullivan>听着，我不是这个镇的人。这与我无关。但我好奇那里是否很多人看了这些报道并翻了白眼。那些不宽恕的人呢？去本顿港看看。他们并不难找。

00:43:35.430 --> 00:43:41.400
<v Robert Walker>Fuck him. Fuck him. I'm serious. I'm good.
<v Robert Walker>去他的。去他的。我是认真的。我很好。

00:43:41.400 --> 00:43:44.850
<v Lilly Sullivan>This is Robert Walker, also known as White Boy Rob.
<v Lilly Sullivan>这是罗伯特·沃克，也叫“白人罗伯”。

00:43:44.850 --> 00:43:46.560
<v Lilly Sullivan>Why do they call you White Boy Rob?
<v Lilly Sullivan>为什么叫你“白人罗伯”？

00:43:46.560 --> 00:43:48.730
<v Robert Walker>Because my mom's white.
<v Robert Walker>因为我妈是白人。

00:43:48.730 --> 00:43:49.420
<v Lilly Sullivan>You don't look white.
<v Lilly Sullivan>你看上去不像白人。

00:43:49.420 --> 00:43:51.790
<v Robert Walker>Why, thank you.
<v Robert Walker>谢谢。

00:43:51.790 --> 00:44:04.780
<v Lilly Sullivan>I met him at his house. Or the truth is, I surprised him at his house. I found him in his backyard, riding his lawnmower in his sweatpants. Rob's definitely noticed that no one's doing a TV story about how he feels about this cop.
<v Lilly Sullivan>我是在他家见的他。或者是事实是，我在他家突然出现。我发现他在后院，穿着运动裤骑着割草机。罗伯肯定注意到了没人在做关于他对这个警察感觉如何的电视报道。

00:44:04.780 --> 00:44:21.680
<v Robert Walker>When they went to the Steve Harvey show, why you ain't take me? You ain't take me because I was going to tell you the motherfucking truth. You-- you hyping up this big thing about one person forgiving you. That ain't the only person that you fucked over.
<v Robert Walker>当他们去史蒂夫·哈维秀的时候，为什么你不带我？你没带我是因为我要告诉你他妈的真相。你——你炒作这一件关于一个人宽恕你的大事。那不是你他妈毁掉的唯一一个人。

00:44:21.680 --> 00:44:30.740
<v Robert Walker>That's not-- and it pisses me off that, you know, y'all kissing buddy buddies, but what about everybody else that you done in? What about everybody else?
<v Robert Walker>这不——这让我很生气，你知道，你们在那亲热，但其他人呢？你对其他被你坑的人做了什么？其他人呢？

00:44:30.740 --> 00:44:52.300
<v Lilly Sullivan>Rob says Collins was so corrupt that people would do all kinds of things just to stay out of his way. For a while, one of the local radio stations, 105.3, would warn people whenever Collins was on duty. This one time, they did a special show-- call in and tell us your Corrupt Collins story. So one day, Rob's at a stoplight and he sees Collins in the lane next to him.
<v Lilly Sullivan>罗伯说柯林斯腐败到人们会做各种事情来避开他。有一阵子，当地电台105.3只要柯林斯执勤就会警告大家。有一次，他们做了一个特别节目——打进来告诉我们你关于腐败柯林斯的故事。有一天，罗伯在红绿灯处看到柯林斯在旁边的车道。

00:44:52.300 --> 00:45:16.740
<v Robert Walker>I was on M139 in Pipestone. I was in a black Impala SS. This is actually like a race car. He was next to me in a Ford Taurus, which is a putt-putt to me. Then when I was at the stop light, I looked over at him. He looked over at me. And then when I turned, he just jumped straight behind me. At this point, I'm thinking that he's going to plant some drugs on me.
<v Robert Walker>我在Pipestone的M139公路上。我开着一辆黑色Impala SS。这其实像辆赛车。他在我旁边开着一辆福特金牛座，对我来说就是辆破车。然后在红绿灯处，我看向他。他看向我。然后当我转弯时，他直接跳到我后面。这时候，我在想他要往我车里塞毒品了。

00:45:16.740 --> 00:45:35.010
<v Lilly Sullivan>Collins throws on his lights and sirens. And Rob's like, I'm not stopping. Instead, he says, he just keeps driving, under the speed limit with Collins following him, lights and sirens on. Rob drives for four minutes like this, all the way to the police station, the only place he thinks he might be safe.
<v Lilly Sullivan>柯林斯打开灯和警笛。罗伯心想，我不停。相反，他说，他只是继续开车，保持在限速以下，柯林斯跟在他后面，灯光和警笛齐鸣。罗伯就这样开了四分钟，一直开到警察局，他认为那里是唯一安全的地方。

00:45:35.010 --> 00:45:41.030
<v Robert Walker>I went to the police station and told them, he want to search my car. Could you please watch him so he won't plant nothing in my car?
<v Robert Walker>我去了警察局告诉他们，他想搜查我的车。能不能请你们盯着他，好让他不会在我车里塞任何东西？

00:45:41.030 --> 00:45:42.330
<v Lilly Sullivan>What did they say to you?
<v Lilly Sullivan>他们跟你说了什么？

00:45:42.330 --> 00:45:51.260
<v Robert Walker>Nothing. They watched. They watched him search. They knew. They knew he was dirty.
<v Robert Walker>什么都没说。他们看着。他们看着他搜查。他们知道。他们知道他是脏的。

00:45:51.260 --> 00:46:02.960
<v Robert Walker>I mean, come on, what guy is going to pull up to the police station, get arrested, and the whole time, I'm not worried about nothing? I'm just worried about him planting something. Y'all watch him and make sure he don't plant nothing.
<v Robert Walker>我的意思是，得了吧，什么人会开到警察局被逮捕，整个过程中我一点都不担心？我只担心他塞东西。你们看着他，确保他没塞任何东西。

00:46:02.960 --> 00:46:23.330
<v Lilly Sullivan>Rob went to jail that day. Not for drugs-- Collins didn't find any. But he arrested Rob anyway for fleeing and eluding. Rob didn't do much time, a couple of days, bonded out $3,000. But he did get convicted. In Michigan, fleeing and eluding is a felony. It's still on his record.
<v Lilly Sullivan>罗伯那天进了监狱。不是因为毒品——柯林斯没搜到任何东西。但柯林斯还是以“逃避和躲避”罪名逮捕了罗伯。罗伯没坐几年牢，几天而已，交了3000美元保释金。但他确实被定罪了。在密歇根州，逃避追捕是重罪。这还在他的记录上。

00:46:23.330 --> 00:46:40.440
<v Lilly Sullivan>This other guy, Quacy Roberts, suffered more. In 2008, Quacy was leaving his girlfriend's house, hot summer day, when Collins and his partner pulled up. The partner said he'd seen Quacy throw something. Collins patted Quacy down while his partner walked around the block.
<v Lilly Sullivan>另一个人，奎西·罗伯茨，受的苦更多。2008年，奎西离开女友家，大热天，柯林斯和搭档开了过来。搭档说看到奎西扔了什么东西。柯林斯搜身奎西，而他的搭档绕着街区走了一圈。

00:46:40.440 --> 00:46:54.580
<v Lilly Sullivan>The partner came back and waved a baggie of crack in Quacy's face. Quacy says it wasn't his, but he ended up serving a year in jail. It was a terrible year to be in jail. He and his girlfriend were about to have a baby. The baby didn't make it.
<v Lilly Sullivan>搭档回来时把一小袋可卡因挥在奎西脸上。奎西说那不是他的，但他最终坐了一年牢。那是极度糟糕的一年。他和女友刚要有一个孩子。那个孩子没保住。

00:46:54.580 --> 00:47:05.210
<v Quacy Roberts>When you have a stillborn, it's really a person. You got to go through all those stages, name it, get a social security number and everything. You got to go through everything. It's a person. You got to bury it.
<v Quacy Roberts>当你有一个死产儿时，他真是一个人。你得经历所有那些阶段，命名他，获得社会保障号码等等。你得经历一切。他是一个人。你得安葬他。

00:47:05.210 --> 00:47:07.630
<v Lilly Sullivan>He still thinks about the baby.
<v Lilly Sullivan>他还在想那个孩子。

00:47:07.630 --> 00:47:13.970
<v Quacy Roberts>Yes, because he was mine.
<v Quacy Roberts>是的，因为他是我的。

00:47:13.970 --> 00:47:24.710
<v Lilly Sullivan>Quacy couldn't be with his girlfriend for any of that. He had to hear about it through the prison phone. Not long after that, his girlfriend met someone else. So he's pretty angry at Collins.
<v Lilly Sullivan>奎西没能陪在女友身边经历那些。他不得不通过监狱电话听到这个消息。不久后，他女友遇见了别人。所以他对柯林斯很生气。

00:47:24.710 --> 00:47:34.520
<v Lilly Sullivan>Quacy's seen those news stories too. His mom called him once and told him to turn on the Megyn Kelly show, one of those stories with Andrew Collins and Jameel McGee, the guy who forgave him.
<v Lilly Sullivan>奎西也看到了那些新闻报道。他妈有次打给他，让他打开梅根·凯利秀，那个关于安德鲁·柯林斯和贾米尔·麦基（那个原谅他的男人）的故事。

00:47:34.520 --> 00:47:44.550
<v Quacy Roberts>It's good that McGee smiling about everything. He must didn't lose nobody. He blessed. I guess he blessed.
<v Quacy Roberts>麦基对一切都在微笑很好。他一定没失去任何人。他有福气。我想他有福气。

00:47:44.550 --> 00:47:45.440
<v Lilly Sullivan>He's blessed?
<v Lilly Sullivan>他有福气？

00:47:45.440 --> 00:48:07.680
<v Quacy Roberts>Yeah, I guess he did four years and didn't lose nobody. I did a year and lost a son. So I'm-- I think an Andrew Collins interview with me would be-- I won't be showing no teeth for grinning at him. Maybe if I wouldn't have lost my child, maybe I'd be smiling too.
<v Quacy Roberts>是啊，我猜他坐了四年牢没失去任何人。我坐了一年失去了儿子。所以我——我想安德鲁·柯林斯采访我不会是——我不会对着他露牙齿笑的。也许如果我没失去孩子，我也能笑。

00:48:07.680 --> 00:48:18.880
<v Lilly Sullivan>Quacy hasn't seen Collins since he went to jail, so Quacy never got an apology. But he thinks he deserves one. I asked if he wanted to meet with Collins. He said yeah.
<v Lilly Sullivan>奎西进监狱后就没见过柯林斯，所以从未收到道歉。但他认为他应得一个。我问他想见柯林斯吗。他说想。

00:48:18.880 --> 00:48:42.740
<v Lilly Sullivan>It was easier to find Collins. He actually has a publicist from when he and Jameel wrote a book together. It came out last year. It's described as "an unlikely journey of forgiveness and friendship." Collins is big-- 6'1", blue eyes, dresses like a surfer who's also on the football team, who's also in a Christian punk band-- flip-flops, shorts, a few tattoos, some with scripture.
<v Lilly Sullivan>找柯林斯更容易。他和贾米尔合写一本书后有了一名公关。那本书去年出版。被描述为“一段关于宽恕和友谊的不太可能的旅程”。柯林斯很强壮——6英尺1英寸，蓝眼睛，穿得像个同时是足球队队员、同时在基督朋克乐队里的冲浪运动员——人字拖，短裤，一些纹身，有些带有经文。

00:48:42.740 --> 00:49:12.130
<v Lilly Sullivan>These days, he freely admits that he'd pull people over without probable cause, write phony police reports, falsify warrants, plant drugs in people's cars. I think one of the ways he's able to sleep at night is that he believes nearly all the people he wrongly arrested were drug dealers. He thought they should be in jail. And about those TV stories, I was surprised, but Collins actually agrees with me. He thinks it's messed up how they all focus on him and Jameel and forgiveness and ignore the harm he did to so many people in the community.
<v Lilly Sullivan>这些天，他自由承认他会无端拦下人们，写虚假的警察报告，伪造搜查令，在人们车里塞毒品。我想他能入睡的原因之一是他认为所有被他错误逮捕的人都是毒贩。他认为他们就该进监狱。关于那些电视故事，我很惊讶，但柯林斯其实同意我的看法。他认为那些报道把重点全部集中在他和贾米尔以及宽恕上，而忽略了他在社区里造成的伤害，这太搞砸了。

00:49:12.130 --> 00:49:29.540
<v Andrew Collins>It's not a story about a community, though. It should be, but it hasn't been. It's been told as a story about Jameel and I, just this fun-loving story, this fun, cute story we can pet. But I don't-- I've never met a reporter that's like, I would like to meet somebody who still hates you.
<v Andrew Collins>不过，这并不是关于一个社区的故事。它本应该是，但它不是。它被讲述成关于贾米尔和我的故事，只是这个充满乐趣的故事，这个我们能随意抚摸的有趣、可爱的故事。但我还没见过一个记者说：“我想见见一个仍然恨你的人。”

00:49:29.540 --> 00:49:48.070
<v Lilly Sullivan>I'm happy to be that reporter. And he said he was game to meet with Quacy. He sees as his responsibility to apologize as much as he can. It's his restitution, he says. And he has a whole way he deals with conversations like this. He's worked out all the words, the best way to do it.
<v Lilly Sullivan>我很乐意做那个记者。他说他愿意和奎西见面。他视向尽可能多的人道歉为自己的责任。他说这是他的赔偿。他有一套处理这类谈话的方法。他已经想好了所有话，最好的表达方式。

00:49:48.070 --> 00:50:01.340
<v Andrew Collins>It's all about owning what you did and giving people a chance to lament the wrong things you did. And then I'll say, you're exactly-- you're absolutely right. I'm so sorry for those things I did.
<v Andrew Collins>全是关于承认你做过的事，并给人们一个机会去哀悼你所做的错误的事情。然后我会说：“你是对的——你绝对是对的。我很抱歉我做的那些事。”

00:50:01.340 --> 00:50:28.110
<v Andrew Collins>And it-- it kind of sucks the anger out of somebody when the offender is standing right in front of you saying, you're right. No, no justification, no minimizing, no-- no reason. I can't give you any reason why I did it. There's no good reason why I did that to you, and I owe you an apology.
<v Andrew Collins>当冒犯者就站在你面前说“你是对的”时，它会吸走一个人的愤怒。没理由，不最小化，不——没理由。我无法给你任何我这样做的理由。我那样对待你没有好理由，我欠你一个道歉。

00:50:28.110 --> 00:50:35.780
<v Lilly Sullivan>Collins suggested that he and Quacy meet at the office of this Christian organization where he volunteers. It had been 10 years since they'd seen each other.
<v Lilly Sullivan>柯林斯建议他和奎西在他担任志愿者的基督教组织办公室见面。那是他们上次见面十年后了。

00:50:35.780 --> 00:50:36.210
<v Andrew Collins>What's up, man?
<v Andrew Collins>怎么了，老兄？

00:50:36.210 --> 00:50:37.500
<v Quacy Roberts>What's going on with you?
<v Quacy Roberts>你最近怎么样？

00:50:37.500 --> 00:50:42.250
<v Andrew Collins>How you doing? Long time no see. You haven't aged a bit, man.
<v Andrew Collins>你最近怎么样？好久不见。你一点都没老，老兄。

00:50:42.250 --> 00:50:45.970
<v Quacy Roberts>No, I have haven't.
<v Quacy Roberts>不，我没有。

00:50:45.970 --> 00:50:47.270
<v Andrew Collins>What they say, black don't crack?
<v Andrew Collins>他们怎么说来着，黑人不会变老？

00:50:47.270 --> 00:50:50.060
<v Quacy Roberts>No, it crack. Especially if you drink a whole lot.
<v Quacy Roberts>不，会变老。尤其是如果你喝太多酒的话。

00:50:50.060 --> 00:50:55.270
<v Andrew Collins>Right. No, man, you look good. I'm not used to seeing you without a hat, though.
<v Andrew Collins>对。没，老兄，你看起来不错。不过我不习惯没戴帽子的你。

00:50:55.270 --> 00:50:59.600
<v Quacy Roberts>Oh, yeah. You is what you is.
<v Quacy Roberts>哦，是的。你是什么样就是什么样。

00:50:59.600 --> 00:51:02.710
<v Andrew Collins>There you go. There you go.
<v Andrew Collins>就是这样。就是这样。

00:51:02.710 --> 00:51:04.540
<v Lilly Sullivan>You guys remember each other now?
<v Lilly Sullivan>你们现在还记得对方吗？

00:51:04.540 --> 00:51:05.310
<v Andrew Collins>Yeah.
<v Andrew Collins>是的。

00:51:05.310 --> 00:51:06.710
<v Quacy Roberts>I remember like it was yesterday.
<v Quacy Roberts>我记得就像昨天一样。

00:51:06.710 --> 00:51:13.440
<v Lilly Sullivan>They sit down. Collins says, I'm trying to remember exactly how I know you. I know we've had plenty of run-ins.
<v Lilly Sullivan>他们坐下。柯林斯说，我试着准确记住我怎么认识你的。我知道我们有很多摩擦。

00:51:13.440 --> 00:51:33.830
<v Andrew Collins>I don't want to say that, like, from the beginning. Like, so it's been 10 years since I've even been there. So if I don't remember specifics, like, that's not to disrespect you or anything like that, because I know this was very personal to you. It's just that I don't remember. There was so many people and I did so many bad things, so.
<v Andrew Collins>我不从头开始说。就像十年没去过那里一样。所以如果我没记住细节，那不是不尊重你，因为我知道这对你非常私人。只是我不记得了。有太多人了，我做了太多坏事，所以。

00:51:33.830 --> 00:51:37.460
<v Quacy Roberts>I'll start to make it specific, because I still have the police report.
<v Quacy Roberts>我会开始说具体点，因为我还有警方报告。

00:51:37.460 --> 00:52:00.770
<v Lilly Sullivan>They talk about the day that Collins and his partner arrested Quacy. Collins' partner was dirty, too. He went to prison after Collins did. And he's actually the one who said he saw Quacy throw the baggie of drugs, which Quacy says he didn't do. Collins says he remembers going back to the spot later to take photos for the trial and realizing his partner was probably lying. He couldn't have seen what he said he saw. There were trees in the way.
<v Lilly Sullivan>他们谈到了柯林斯和搭档逮捕奎西的那天。他的搭档也是个坏警察。他在柯林斯之后进了监狱。他其实才是那个说看到奎西扔毒品的人，奎西说他没干。柯林斯说他记得后来回到那个地点为审判拍照，意识到他的搭档可能撒谎了。他不可能看到他所说的情况。中间有树挡着。

00:52:00.770 --> 00:52:08.060
<v Andrew Collins>So I remember thinking then, like, OK, my partner might have stretched the truth a little bit. But I still was convinced you were guilty because he said you were guilty, right? So--
<v Andrew Collins>所以我记得当时想，好吧，我的搭档可能夸大了事实。但我还是确信你是内疚的，因为他说你内疚，对吧？所以——

00:52:08.060 --> 00:52:09.200
<v Quacy Roberts>It's what the police say.
<v Quacy Roberts>那是警察说的。

00:52:09.200 --> 00:52:13.100
<v Andrew Collins>I know I wanted you, though. I know I wanted you to be guilty.
<v Andrew Collins>我知道我想要你，虽然。我知道我想要你有罪。

00:52:13.100 --> 00:52:14.460
<v Lilly Sullivan>Why?
<v Lilly Sullivan>为什么？

00:52:14.460 --> 00:52:20.590
<v Andrew Collins>Quacy bothered me when I was a police officer, because he didn't-- he didn't just shut up. Like, he'd tell me my breath stunk, and--
<v Andrew Collins>奎西当我做警察时惹恼了我，因为他不会——他不会闭嘴。比如，他会告诉我的呼吸很臭，然后——

00:52:20.590 --> 00:52:22.280
<v Quacy Roberts>I'd never--
<v Quacy Roberts>我从来没——

00:52:22.280 --> 00:52:30.140
<v Lilly Sullivan>Quacy thought Collins had gotten off way too easy for what he did. He only spent a year and a half in jail. Colin says he hears that a lot.
<v Lilly Sullivan>奎西认为柯林斯因为他做的事而过得太轻松了。他只在监狱里待了一年半。柯林斯说他经常听到这个。

00:52:30.140 --> 00:52:38.010
<v Andrew Collins>I think there are some people that would say, day for day, whatever those other people did in jail, he should have to do that now-- day for day.
<v Andrew Collins>我想有些人会说，日复一日，不管那些人在监狱里做了什么，他现在也该做到——日复一日。

00:52:38.010 --> 00:52:58.790
<v Quacy Roberts>Right. That would have sounded good. That would have been fair. Like whatever, you can set it by saying, one person got two to five. Another person got 40-something months to something months. Take all them, add all of them together, and see can you face up to them? Bad mofo, ooh. Yeah, yeah, that'd be appropriate. Yeah.
<v Quacy Roberts>对。那听起来不错。那会很公平。就像无论什么，你可以通过说“一个人得到了两到五年，另一个人得到了40多个月”来设定。把它们全加上，看看能不能面对它们？坏家伙，哦。是的，是的，那很恰当。

00:52:58.790 --> 00:53:01.460
<v Andrew Collins>I'm glad it didn't happen. But I could see that argument.
<v Andrew Collins>很高兴那没发生。但我能理解那个论点。

00:53:01.460 --> 00:53:02.960
<v Quacy Roberts>Yeah.
<v Quacy Roberts>是啊。

00:53:02.960 --> 00:53:12.890
<v Lilly Sullivan>A lot of the conversation was awkward. Collins took a long time to get to his apology, and when he did, it was kind of indirect. It seems like Quacy barely even noticed.
<v Lilly Sullivan>大部分谈话很尴尬。柯林斯花了好长时间才说到道歉，即使说了，也比较间接。奎西似乎几乎没注意到。

00:53:12.890 --> 00:53:37.950
<v Andrew Collins>Even though I wasn't the one that said I saw you throw it, it was my influence in that situation that brought my partner to a place where he felt comfortable saying those lies, if they were lies, right? So therefore, I do owe you an- I believe, man to man, I owe you an apology because I operated in the system that's broken. And I helped make it--
<v Andrew Collins>即使不是我说看到你扔的，也是我在那个情况下的影响把我的搭档带到了一个他觉得可以说谎（如果是谎言的话）的位置，对吧？所以我认为，男人对男人，我欠你一个道歉，因为我在一个破碎的系统中运作。我帮忙让它——

00:53:37.950 --> 00:53:38.410
<v Quacy Roberts>So what you--
<v Quacy Roberts>所以你——

00:53:38.410 --> 00:53:39.310
<v Andrew Collins>--even more broken.
<v Andrew Collins>——更破碎了。

00:53:39.310 --> 00:53:42.460
<v Quacy Roberts>So what you saying is, you could have just been more blunt like that.
<v Quacy Roberts>所以你是说，你本可以更直截了当地那样说。

00:53:42.460 --> 00:54:01.700
<v Lilly Sullivan>What got to Quacy more than the apology was something that they saw eye to eye on, and kind of bonded over. For most of the hour, they talked about how bad the policing is in Benton Harbor. Benton Harbor is 85% black with a police department that's mostly white that has a history of misconduct. There have been decades of protests against the police and a few riots.
<v Lilly Sullivan>比道歉更能打动奎西的是他们的共识，并因此有了共鸣。大部分时间，他们都在谈论本顿港的警察有多糟糕。本顿港85%是黑人，警察部门大部分是白人，有不当行为的历史。几十年来一直在抗议警察，发生过几次暴乱。

00:54:01.700 --> 00:54:20.250
<v Lilly Sullivan>Collins has named names in court depositions, talked about how many officers were complicit in bad policing. He spends a lot of his book detailing illegal behavior by cops that he says he witnessed and participated in. He talks to police trainees about corruption and racism and how easy it is to slip into those things.
<v Lilly Sullivan>柯林斯在法庭证词中指名道姓，谈论了多少警官共谋了糟糕的警务。他花了很多书的篇幅详细叙述了他目睹并参与的警察非法行为。他告诉警察受训人员关于腐败和种族歧视，以及陷入这些事情有多容易。

00:54:20.250 --> 00:54:30.950
<v Lilly Sullivan>So he and Quacy got into all of that. They know the same stories, same cops and judges and prosecutors, people they both believe to be crooked who never got caught. There's lots of insider talk.
<v Lilly Sullivan>所以他和奎西深入探讨了这一切。他们知道同样的故事，同样的警察、法官和检察官，他们都认为那些人是骗子，但从没被抓住。有很多内部交谈。

00:54:30.950 --> 00:54:31.370
<v Andrew Collins>Right.
<v Andrew Collins>对。

00:54:31.370 --> 00:54:38.520
<v Quacy Roberts>You see Officer [BLEEP] on film. I'm telling them, I want to subpoena Officer [BLEEP]. Oh, we can't find him. We don't know where he at.
<v Quacy Roberts>你看到警官[哔]在电影上。我告诉他们，我想传唤警官[哔]。哦，我们找不到他。我们不知道他在哪。

00:54:38.520 --> 00:54:40.820
<v Andrew Collins>Yeah. He still works there, don't he?
<v Andrew Collins>对。他还在那工作，不是吗？

00:54:40.820 --> 00:54:41.340
<v Quacy Roberts>Right.
<v Quacy Roberts>对。

00:54:41.340 --> 00:55:03.470
<v Andrew Collins>That's crazy, man. Different officer, same candy wrapper. But the system itself is-- man, it's just full of corruption. You know, you see corruption day in and day out. You stop viewing people as human. You start to see them as, you know, the next prize, the next notch in my belt, in my career, right?
<v Andrew Collins>那太疯狂了，老兄。不同的警察，同样的糖纸。但系统本身——男人，它充满了腐败。你知道，你日复一日地看到腐败。你不再把人看作人类。你开始把他们看作，你知道，下一个奖品，我在我职业生涯中的下一个记录，对吧？

00:55:03.470 --> 00:55:08.690
<v Lilly Sullivan>Collins really was trying to connect and win Quacy over. He even offered to help Quacy with a case he has pending.
<v Lilly Sullivan>柯林斯确实在试图联系并赢得奎西。他甚至提出帮奎西处理他手头的一个案子。

00:55:08.690 --> 00:55:22.010
<v Andrew Collins>You want me to take a copy of the report? Well, I mean, if you want to, give me the report. Let me look through it. Trust. I'm not a lawyer, but I've seen crooked police work before. Let me look through it and just show you like, hey, fight this, fight this, fight this.
<v Andrew Collins>你想让我拿一份报告复印件吗？好吧，我的意思是，如果你想的话，给我报告。让我看看。信任。我不是律师，但我之前见过卑鄙的警察工作。让我看看它，给你指出，嘿，反击这个，反击这个，反击这个。

00:55:22.010 --> 00:55:30.950
<v Lilly Sullivan>They seem to get along fine. They were polite enough. Quacy seemed like, whatever. An ex-cop who says he's on your side? It can't hurt.
<v Lilly Sullivan>他们似乎相处得很好。他们足够客气。奎西似乎觉得，无所谓了。一个说站在你这一边的前警察？没坏处。

00:55:30.950 --> 00:55:35.710
<v Lilly Sullivan>After it was over, I walked with Quacy to his car.
<v Lilly Sullivan>结束后，我和奎西一起走到他的车前。

00:55:35.710 --> 00:55:38.480
<v Lilly Sullivan>So how are you feeling?
<v Lilly Sullivan>所以你感觉如何？

00:55:38.480 --> 00:55:41.050
<v Quacy Roberts>I'm feeling all right.
<v Quacy Roberts>我觉得还可以。

00:55:41.050 --> 00:55:44.050
<v Lilly Sullivan>You're not mad at him?
<v Lilly Sullivan>你不生他的气？

00:55:44.050 --> 00:55:49.040
<v Quacy Roberts>How can you get mad to a person that we ain't even at the bottom of the situation yet?
<v Quacy Roberts>你怎能对一个我们还没到情况底层的人感到生气？

00:55:49.040 --> 00:56:02.700
<v Lilly Sullivan>Meaning, Collins didn't invent dirty policing, but he admitted to it. Collins confirmed everything Quacy already believed about the courts and prosecutors and judges, and about the Benton Harbor police who usually deny everything.
<v Lilly Sullivan>意思是，柯林斯没发明卑鄙的警务，但他承认了。柯林斯证实了奎西已经相信的关于法院、检察官和法官，以及通常否认一切的本顿港警察的一切。

00:56:02.700 --> 00:56:30.050
<v Quacy Roberts>But by us talking, we find out, 10 years later, that he on the same page that I'm on, fighting against the same people that I'm fighting against. He looking at the police point of view in the system. I'm looking at the street point of view of the system. So both of us looking at it both ways, his way and my way. And I'm looking at it my way and his way.
<v Quacy Roberts>但通过谈话，我们发现，10年后，他和我在同一页面上，反抗着我反抗着的同样的人。他看着系统中警察的观点。我看着系统中街道的观点。所以我们俩都以两种方式看着它，他的方式和我的方式。我以我的方式和他的方式看着它。

00:56:30.050 --> 00:56:34.580
<v Lilly Sullivan>Rob Walker, who used to be known as White Boy Rob, he sees things differently.
<v Lilly Sullivan>罗伯特·沃克，曾经被称为“白人罗伯”，他看待事物的方式不同。

00:56:34.580 --> 00:56:44.120
<v Robert Walker>At this point in my life, if he did apologize, it don't mean shit. Honestly? I just want one chance to whoop his ass.
<v Robert Walker>在我人生的这个阶段，如果他道歉了，那也一点意义没有。老实说？我只想要一次机会揍他一顿。

00:56:44.120 --> 00:56:53.850
<v Lilly Sullivan>As I said, he didn't want to talk with Collins. But he told me they did bump into each other accidentally. It's a small town. It happened at that cafe from the news story.
<v Lilly Sullivan>如我所说，他不想和柯林斯谈。但他告诉我他们偶然撞见了对方。那是个小镇。这发生在新闻报道里的那家咖啡馆。

00:56:53.850 --> 00:57:08.470
<v Robert Walker>So when I see him, I said-- I said, Collins. And he looked. And when he looked at me, I said, man, you've been on some bullshit. He want to talk about how that ain't him no more. Well, I told him, I don't want any of that shit.
<v Robert Walker>所以当我看到他时，我说——我说，柯林斯。他看了看。当他看着我时，我说，老兄，你一直在搞些狗屁。他想谈谈那不再是他了。嗯，我告诉他，我不想听那些狗屁。

00:57:08.470 --> 00:57:21.840
<v Robert Walker>That don't help me. It don't help me that you want to-- all of the sudden you want to change. Oh, let me ask you-- I'm going to be totally honest with you. Did he understand that I changed?
<v Robert Walker>那对我没帮助。你想要——突然你想要改变，那对我没帮助。哦，让我问你——我要对你非常诚实。他理解我改变了吗？

00:57:21.840 --> 00:57:36.600
<v Lilly Sullivan>He'd changed a lot. Back when Collins arrested him, he'd been away from drugs for years. Everyone knew that, he says. He even put on his license plate, game over. And it was on his car that day that Collins pulled him over.
<v Lilly Sullivan>他改变了很多。柯林斯逮捕他时，他已经远离毒品好几年了。每个人都知道，他说。他甚至在车牌上写了“游戏结束”。那天柯林斯拦下他时，那就在他车上。

00:57:36.600 --> 00:57:47.670
<v Lilly Sullivan>Rob's gotten plaques from the city for his community work. He started an annual picnic with free rides and food, paid for it all out of pocket. But he still has a felony on his record from that run-in with Collins.
<v Lilly Sullivan>罗伯因社区工作获得了城市的奖牌。他开始了一年一度的野餐，免费乘车和食物，全是自己掏钱。但他因那次与柯林斯的冲突在记录上还有一个重罪。

00:57:47.670 --> 00:57:59.090
<v Robert Walker>So why should I understand he changed? I shouldn't. I should get to treat you the same way you treat me. You never understood that I changed, that that wasn't my life no more. Now that you claiming that you changed, I feel the same way.
<v Robert Walker>所以为什么我应该理解他改变了？我不应该。我应该能像你对待我一样对待你。你从没理解我改变了，那不再是我的生活了。现在你声称你改变了，我感觉一样。

00:57:59.090 --> 00:58:12.780
<v Robert Walker>You still a dirty motherfucker to me. I don't care what did you say. If Collins had a chance to do it all over again, he'd do it all over again. Dirty cop-- he was a dirty cop, he is. And to me, that forgiveness shit is overrated.
<v Robert Walker>对我来说你还是个卑鄙的混蛋。我不管你说了什么。如果柯林斯有机会重来一遍，他还是会重来一遍。卑鄙警察——他是个卑鄙警察，他是。对我来说，那个宽恕的狗屁被高估了。

00:58:12.780 --> 00:58:17.010
<v Lilly Sullivan>A takeaway which, of course, is the opposite of all of those news stories.
<v Lilly Sullivan>这是一个收获，当然，与所有那些新闻故事相反。

00:58:17.010 --> 00:58:19.850
<v Lilly Sullivan>Is there a lesson for America from this?
<v Lilly Sullivan>这对美国有什么教训吗？

00:58:19.850 --> 00:58:23.270
<v Robert Walker>No.
<v Robert Walker>没有。

00:58:23.270 --> 00:58:44.910
<v Lilly Sullivan>The story of someone who doesn't forgive, who thinks being angry is a totally reasonable way to feel. Yeah, maybe it's not heartwarming. But what's wrong with bearing a grudge forever? It could be something to hold onto that keeps you sane.
<v Lilly Sullivan>一个不宽恕的人的故事，一个认为愤怒是一个完全合理的感受方式的人的故事。是的，也许它不暖心。但永远怀恨在心有什么错？它可能是可以坚持的东西，让你保持理智。

00:58:44.910 --> 00:58:48.080
<v Ira Glass>Lilly Sullivan is one of the producers of our show.
<v Ira Glass>莉莉·沙利文是我们节目的制片人之一。

00:58:48.080 --> 00:58:48.350
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "MAKE A CHANGE" BY DURAND JONES & THE INDICATIONS]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - “MAKE A CHANGE” BY DURAND JONES & THE INDICATIONS]

00:58:48.350 --> 00:59:12.080
<v Woman>(SINGING) To make you want me I can fabricate the truth. I'll give you easy, it'll keep me destitute. You hang me up on the line, hang me out to dry, and you got nothing to lose.
<v Woman>（歌唱）为了让你想要我，我可以编造真相。我给你容易的，它会让我赤贫。你把我挂在线上，把我挂出来晾干，你没什么可失去的。

